nech Cienfuegos I is a researcher involved in the study of dietary-therapeutic products.ARENCIBIA RIVERO T:Arencibia Rivero T is a researcher contributing to the study of dietary-therapeutic products.MANZANO MART√çN MV:Manzano Mart√≠n MV is a researcher involved in the study of dietary-therapeutic products.PANCOBO-HIDALGO PL:Pancorbo-Hidalgo PL is a researcher studying complications associated with enteral nutrition.GARIN GROUP ANDALUSIAN GROUP FOR NUTRITION REFLECTION AND INVESTIGATION:The GARIN Group is an organization focused on nutrition research and recommendations for managing patients with diabetes or stress hyperglycemia and artificial nutrition.FRANCISCO VILLAZ√ìN GONZ√ÅLEZ:Dr. Francisco Villaz√≥n Gonz√°lez is a medical specialist in endocrinology and nutrition, recognized for his contributions to the understanding of diabetes mellitus in Spain. He serves as a specialist in the Nutrition Section at the University Hospital Central of Asturias, located in Oviedo, Asturias.CIBERDEM:CIBERDEM, officially known as the Centro de Investigaci√≥n Biom√©dica en Red de Diabetes y Enfermedades Metab√≥licas Asociadas, is a prominent biomedical research center dedicated to the study of diabetes and associated metabolic diseases. This center has conducted significant research, including a study on the incidence of diabetes in Spain, highlighting its commitment to understanding and addressing the challenges posed by these health issues. CIBERDEM plays a crucial role in advancing knowledge and developing solutions related to diabetes and its related conditions.DARIOS STUDY:The DARIOS study is a research project that reviewed multiple studies on cardiovascular risk factors in Spain, revealing a diabetes prevalence of 13%.DI@BET STUDY:The Di@bet study was conducted between 2008 and 2010, assessing diabetes prevalence among the Spanish population, finding a 13.8% prevalence rate.OVIEDO:Oviedo is the capital city of the province of Asturias in Spain. It is notable for being the location of the Hospital Universitario Central de Asturias, a significant healthcare facility in the region.SECCAID:SECCAID is a study that assessed the direct annual costs of diabetes in Spain.DISLIPEMIA:Dyslipidemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormal lipid levels in the blood, which is part of the patient's medical history. This condition is a significant risk factor for cardiovascular disease and can be influenced by various factors, including diet and obesity. Dyslipidemia is also associated with diabetes, contributing to potential cardiovascular complications. It is important to note that dyslipidemia can complicate the treatment of patients, particularly those who are obese. Overall, dyslipidemia represents a disorder of lipid metabolism that requires careful management to mitigate its associated health risks.SISTEMAS DE SALUD:Health systems in Spain are responsible for managing diabetes care and prevention, impacting public health budgets.PROVINCIA:Provinces in Spain show varying mortality rates related to diabetes, with some regions experiencing higher rates.COMUNIDAD VALENCIANA:The Comunidad Valenciana is an autonomous community located in Spain. It is notable for a study conducted on the prevalence of diabetes, which revealed a prevalence rate of 14%. In addition to its health statistics, the Comunidad Valenciana has specific regulations regarding patient care; individuals requiring ongoing treatment for nutritional needs must submit a justification report to continue their treatment.ESTUDIO DI@BET.ES:A study that shows a diabetes prevalence of 13.8% in the population, with a significant portion undiagnosed.COSTE DE LA DIABETES:COSTE DE LA DIABETES refers to the economic impact and healthcare costs associated with diabetes management and treatment. The economic cost of diabetes and its interventions is significant, estimated to reach 11.6% of global health expenditure. This issue is particularly notable in Spain, where the financial burden of diabetes is felt acutely, highlighting the importance of addressing the economic implications of this chronic condition.PREVALENCIA DE DIABETES:The prevalence of diabetes in the adult population in Spain is reported to be 13.8%, with a higher rate among older adults.DEPARTAMENTO DE SALUD LA FE:The Departamento de Salud La Fe is a health department in the Comunidad Valenciana that focuses on diabetes management and health interventions.MEDICINA DE PRECISI√ìN:Medicina de Precisi√≥n refers to a modern approach in healthcare that tailors medical treatment to the individual characteristics of each patient.SIP (SISTEMA D'INFORMACI√ì POPULACIONAL):SIP is the identification system used in the Comunidad Valenciana to manage health data.ALCOI:Alcoi is a municipality in the Comunidad Valenciana that is part of the diabetes management program.ALICANTE-HOSPITAL GENERAL:Alicante-Hospital General is a general hospital situated in Alicante, Spain, and is an integral part of the health system in the Comunidad Valenciana. This healthcare facility serves the local population by offering a wide range of medical services, ensuring access to essential healthcare for the community.ALICANTE-SANT JOAN D'ALACANT:Alicante-Sant Joan d'Alacant is a healthcare facility located in the Alicante region of the Comunidad Valenciana. It provides a range of medical services, with a particular focus on diabetes care, among other healthcare offerings.PLATAFORMA TIC EN SALUD:The Plataforma TIC en Salud is a technological platform developed for health data analysis and management in the Comunidad Valenciana.ANATOM√çA PATOL√ìGICA:Anatom√≠a Patol√≥gica refers to the study of diseases through the examination of tissues, playing a crucial role in the analysis of health data. This field encompasses the investigation of various pathological conditions, including the confirmation of specific diagnoses. For instance, a recent pathological anatomy report confirmed the diagnosis of neoplasia intraductal papilar mucinosa, highlighting the importance of Anatom√≠a Patol√≥gica in understanding and diagnosing diseases.RADIOLOG√çA:Radiolog√≠a involves imaging techniques used to diagnose and monitor diseases, including diabetes-related complications.LABORATORIO:Laboratorio refers to medical laboratories that conduct tests and analyses related to health conditions, including diabetes.INTERVENCIONES EN SALUD:Intervenciones en Salud refers to health interventions aimed at preventing and managing diseases like diabetes.H√ÅBITOS ALIMENTARIOS:"H√ÅBITOS ALIMENTARIOS" refers to the dietary practices and consumption behaviors of individuals, which play a significant role in health management and the risk of chronic diseases. These eating habits are patterns of food consumption that can influence the risk and management of conditions such as diabetes. The assessment of "h√°bitos alimentarios" is crucial for understanding nutritional patterns and their associations with health outcomes. In recent years, there has been a dramatic shift in eating habits, characterized by an increase in high-fat diets, which contribute to the development of metabolic diseases. Overall, "h√°bitos alimentarios" encompass the dietary patterns and practices that are essential for nutritional assessments and health management.ALICANTE (DEPARTAMENTO):Alicante (Departamento) refers to the administrative region in Spain that includes the city of Alicante and its surrounding areas.CASTELLON:Castellon is a province in the Valencian Community of Spain, located to the north of Alicante.VALENCIA-HOSPITAL GENERAL:Valencia-Hospital General is a major hospital in Valencia, providing comprehensive healthcare services.VALENCIA-LA FE:Valencia-La Fe is a prominent hospital in Valencia known for its advanced medical care and research.MANISES:Manises is a municipality in the province of Valencia, Spain, known for its historical significance and proximity to Valencia city.VALENCIA-DOCTOR PESET:Valencia-Doctor Peset is a hospital in Valencia that offers a range of medical services to the community.ELDA:Elda is a city in the province of Alicante, Spain, known for its shoe industry and cultural heritage.VALENCIA (DEPARTAMENTO):CRIBADO DE DIABETES:Cribado de diabetes refers to the screening process for diabetes in the population, with a reported percentage of 74.35% in the figure.ENFERMEDAD RENAL:ENFERMEDAD RENAL es una condici√≥n m√©dica que afecta la funci√≥n de los ri√±ones, lo que puede llevar a complicaciones significativas en la salud de los pacientes. Esta enfermedad es especialmente relevante para aquellos que padecen diabetes, ya que puede ser una complicaci√≥n asociada a esta condici√≥n. La enfermedad renal se caracteriza por un aumento del volumen de agua y sodio en el organismo, lo que a su vez requiere la implementaci√≥n de dietas espec√≠ficas, incluyendo una restricci√≥n diet√©tica de sodio, para controlar la hipertensi√≥n arterial y manejar adecuadamente la enfermedad.INERCIA TERAP√âUTICA:Inercia terap√©utica refers to the therapeutic inertia in diabetes treatment, highlighting the delay in prescribing optimal treatments for patients.EMPA-REG STUDY:The EMPA-REG Study is a clinical trial that investigated the cardiovascular safety of a diabetes medication, influencing treatment guidelines.LEADER STUDY:The LEADER Study is a long-term clinical trial designed to assess the cardiovascular outcomes and safety of liraglutide, a medication used in the treatment of diabetes. This study has significant implications for treatment practices, as it evaluates how liraglutide affects cardiovascular health in patients with diabetes. Through its findings, the LEADER Study aims to provide valuable insights into the management of diabetes and its associated cardiovascular risks.EUROASPIRE V:EUROASPIRE V is a study that evaluates the management of cardiovascular disease and diabetes in Europe, providing insights into treatment practices.)<|COMPLETE|>MESA REDONDA DE CASOS CL√çNICOS:The "MESA REDONDA DE CASOS CL√çNICOS" is a round table event focused on discussing clinical cases within the medical community. It features various medical professionals who present and analyze different cases. The event has taken place in the Comunidad Valenciana, where a diverse group of specialists share their insights. Additionally, there is a specific iteration of this event that addresses clinical cases related to diabetes and nutrition, which was held in Granada, also involving various specialists. Overall, the MESA REDONDA DE CASOS CL√çNICOS serves as a platform for collaboration and knowledge exchange among healthcare professionals across different regions and specialties.S√çNDROME MELAS:A rare genetic disorder discussed in one of the clinical cases, characterized by mitochondrial dysfunction and associated with diabetes.NEOPLASIA INTRADUCTAL PAPILAR MUCINOSA:Neoplasia intraductal papilar mucinosa is a specific type of pancreatic tumor that has been discussed in clinical cases. It is associated with malnutrition and diabetes, highlighting its potential impact on the patient's overall health. The patient in the case was diagnosed with this type of tumor, indicating its clinical significance and the need for careful management of related health issues.HOSPITAL GENERAL UNIVERSITARIO SANTA LUC√çA:Hospital General Universitario Santa Luc√≠a is a healthcare institution in Cartagena where specialized medical care is provided, including for patients with endocrine and nutritional needs.S√çNDROME DE MELAS:MELAS is a mitochondrial disease characterized by encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes, significantly affecting muscle and nervous system function, often leading to complications such as diabetes mellitus.ENFERMEDADES MITOCONDRIALES:Mitochondrial diseases are a group of disorders caused by dysfunctional mitochondria, which can lead to a variety of health issues, including metabolic and neurological problems.MALNUTRICI√ìN CAL√ìRICA SEVERA:Severe caloric malnutrition is a condition resulting from inadequate intake of calories, leading to significant health issues, particularly in patients with complex medical conditions like MELAS.POLINEUROPAT√çA SENSITIVO-MOTORA:POLINEUROPAT√çA SENSITIVO-MOTORA, also known as sensory-motor polyneuropathy, is a medical condition that impacts the peripheral nerves. This condition is characterized by both sensory loss and motor weakness, which can significantly affect a patient's quality of life. It is often observed in individuals with mitochondrial diseases, highlighting a potential link between these two health issues. Overall, sensory-motor polyneuropathy represents a serious neurological disorder that requires careful management and treatment.CARCINOMA MUCINOSO DE P√ÅNCREAS:Mucinous pancreatic carcinoma is a type of cancer that affects the pancreas, characterized by the production of mucin, and may require surgical intervention like duodenopancreatectomy.MELAS:MELAS, which stands for Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes, is the most common mitochondrial disease. It is characterized by a heterogeneous phenotypic expression that includes a variety of symptoms affecting multiple systems in the body. Individuals with MELAS often experience neurological impairments, such as migraines, seizures, and episodes resembling strokes. Additionally, the disorder can lead to muscular weakness and various gastrointestinal issues, which frequently result in malnutrition. Other systemic manifestations may include diabetes mellitus. The complexity of MELAS symptoms can vary significantly among affected individuals, making diagnosis and management challenging. One patient was diagnosed with MELAS in 2008, highlighting the ongoing recognition and understanding of this condition.CITRULINA:Citrulina is a non-essential amino acid that plays a significant role in evaluating intestinal health, particularly in determining the need for long-term nutritional support. It serves as a biochemical marker in this context. Additionally, Citrulina is recognized as a supplement that enhances the production of nitric oxide (NO), which is crucial for various physiological functions. It is noted for potentially having better absorption and bioavailability compared to arginine. While Citrulina is relevant in the context of MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes), particularly for patients with low levels of nitric oxide, its specific effects on this condition have not been extensively studied. Overall, Citrulina is an important amino acid with implications for both intestinal health and nitric oxide production.CITOCROMO C:Citocromo C is an enzyme that has a high affinity for nitric oxide and is implicated in the pathophysiology of MELAS due to its interaction with nitric oxide synthase.EPILESIA:Epilepsy is a neurological manifestation observed in a significant percentage of MELAS patients, contributing to the overall clinical picture of the syndrome.NEUROPAT√çA PERIF√âRICA:Neuropat√≠a perif√©rica is a condition affecting the peripheral nerves, noted in MELAS patients, contributing to their neurological symptoms.√ìXIDO N√çTRICO:√ìxido n√≠trico is a crucial signaling molecule that plays a significant role in vascular relaxation and is involved in the pathophysiology of MELAS, particularly through its interaction with endothelial cells. It functions as an endogenous vasodilator, regulating blood pressure and organ perfusion. √ìxido n√≠trico is produced from the amino acid arginine, highlighting its importance in cardiovascular health and disease mechanisms.CITOCROMO C OXIDASA:Citocromo C oxidasa is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in the electron transport chain, which is essential for cellular respiration. This enzyme is particularly noted for its increased activity in patients with MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes), a condition that can impact the availability of nitric oxide in the body.HIPOACUSIA NEUROSENSORIAL:Hipoacusia neurosensorial is a type of hearing loss that is commonly observed in patients with MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes). This auditory manifestation significantly contributes to the overall neurological symptoms associated with the syndrome, thereby affecting the quality of life of those affected. Additionally, it is noted that hipoacusia neurosensorial can also be experienced by family members, such as the patient's mother, indicating a potential genetic link related to the underlying mutation.DEMANDA DE INSULINA:Demanda de insulina refers to the requirement for insulin treatment in MELAS patients, particularly in those developing diabetes mellitus.NEW CASTLE:New Castle is associated with the scale of severity for adult mitochondrial diseases, specifically the NMDAS used to assess patients with MELAS.COENZIMA Q10:Coenzima Q10 is a compound known for its role in stabilizing the electron transport chain and exhibiting antioxidant properties. It is particularly noted for its potential benefits for patients with MELAS (Mitochondrial Encephalomyopathy, Lactic Acidosis, and Stroke-like episodes). Additionally, Coenzima Q10 is utilized as an antioxidant to enhance recovery following exercise, highlighting its versatility in both medical and athletic contexts.DIETA CETOG√âNICA:The "Dieta cetog√©nica" (ketogenic diet) is a dietary approach characterized by a high intake of fats and a significant reduction in carbohydrates. It is primarily utilized for the treatment of refractory epilepsy, particularly in children, and has shown effectiveness in reducing epileptic seizures, including in patients with MELAS syndrome. While the diet provides an alternative energy substrate for the brain, its long-term safety and effectiveness remain uncertain.

The ketogenic diet is often implemented as a treatment for patients who do not respond to conventional medication, and it may have implications for growth, as well as potential digestive and cardiac complications. The diet is typically maintained as long as it continues to be beneficial, with a gradual adjustment process when discontinuing treatment. Various forms and protocols of the ketogenic diet exist, tailored to meet the specific needs of patients, particularly those with intractable epilepsy and other metabolic disorders.DEMANCIAS PSIQUI√ÅTRICAS:Demanias psiqui√°tricas refer to psychiatric manifestations that may arise in patients with MELAS, impacting their mental health.MIOTOP√çA:Miopat√≠a is a cardinal manifestation of MELAS, characterized by muscle weakness and intolerance to exercise.MIOCARDIOPAT√çA HIPERTR√ìFICA:Miocardiopat√≠a hipertr√≥fica is a cardiac manifestation that can occur in MELAS patients, affecting heart function.FALLO DE MEDRO:Fallo de medro is a condition of poor growth that can occur in MELAS patients, often linked to nutritional issues.L-CARNITINE:L-Carnitine is a supplement that plays a crucial role in fatty acid metabolism and is included in treatment regimens to potentially enhance mitochondrial function. It is particularly suggested for patients with primary systemic carnitine deficiency, where it is recognized for its safety and effectiveness in correcting underlying carnitine deficits.COENZYME Q10:Coenzyme Q10 is a compound involved in energy production in cells, used in the patient's treatment to address complications related to MELAS.CLOPIDOGREL:Clopidogrel is a medication used in the patient's treatment, likely for its antiplatelet effects.LACOSAMIDE:Lacosamide is an anticonvulsant medication included in the patient's treatment to manage seizures.RANITIDINE:Ranitidine is a medication used to reduce stomach acid, prescribed to the patient for gastrointestinal symptoms.LEVETIRACETAM:Levetiracetam is an anticonvulsant medication used in the patient's treatment to control seizures.LAMOTRIGINE:Lamotrigine is an anticonvulsant medication included in the patient's treatment plan for seizure management.DOMPERIDONE:Domperidone is a medication used to relieve nausea and vomiting, prescribed to the patient to manage gastrointestinal symptoms.LACTITOL:Lactitol is a laxative used in the patient's treatment to manage constipation.CRISIS CONVULSIVAS:Crisis convulsivas refers to the repeated generalized seizures experienced by the patient.VOMITOS:VOMITOS refers to episodes of vomiting that can manifest in various medical contexts. In children, v√≥mitos can be a symptom associated with classic celiac disease. Additionally, in patients with hyponatremia, v√≥mitos are categorized as moderate neurological symptoms, indicating a potential underlying issue that may require medical attention. Furthermore, episodes of v√≥mitos can significantly impact a patient's treatment compliance, suggesting that managing this symptom is crucial for effective healthcare outcomes.BAJA TALLA:Baja talla refers to the short stature experienced by the patient during childhood, part of her endocrine issues.DIFICULTADES EN LAS ACTIVIDADES BASALES:Dificultades en las actividades basales refers to the patient's dependency for daily living activities due to her health condition.GASTRITIS ATR√ìFICA:Gastritis atr√≥fica is a medical condition characterized by inflammation and thinning of the stomach lining. This condition can lead to impaired absorption of essential nutrients, including iron. The diagnosis of gastritis atr√≥fica indicates that the patient is experiencing these inflammatory changes in the stomach lining, which may have significant implications for their overall health and nutritional status.POLIPOS MULTIPLES:P√≥lipos m√∫ltiples are growths found in the patient's gastrointestinal tract, contributing to her digestive issues.ELECTROLITOS ANORMALES:Electrolitos anormales refers to abnormal electrolyte levels found in the patient's clinical tests.MUTACI√ìN A243G:Mutaci√≥n A243G is a genetic mutation associated with the patient's mother's health issues and the patient's MELAS diagnosis.)<|COMPLETE|>CLOSTRIDIUM DIFFICILE:Clostridium difficile is a bacterium known for causing infections in the intestines, which often lead to severe diarrhea and colitis. This condition is particularly prevalent in patients who have recently undergone antibiotic treatment, as the bacterium can proliferate in the gut following the disruption of normal flora. Testing for the toxin produced by Clostridium difficile is commonly performed in patients presenting with gastrointestinal symptoms, especially diarrhea. The bacterium remains a significant focus of ongoing research aimed at developing effective treatment options.P√âPTIDO C:Peptide C is a marker measured in the patient's study to assess insulin production.TABLA 4:TABLA 4 is a comprehensive table that encompasses a variety of important characteristics related to dietary assessment methods and nutritional guidelines. It presents strategies aimed at reducing the risk of aspiration in patients receiving enteral nutrition. Additionally, TABLA 4 outlines commercial preparations of parenteral nutrition, specifically focusing on ternary mixtures. The table details the energy requirements for girls at three levels of physical activity, providing insights into caloric intake based on age and weight. It also includes reference intake values for an average adult and offers estimations of weight and height derived from other anthropometric parameters. Furthermore, TABLA 4 displays data from a population study concerning body mass index (IMC) for men and women over the age of 60. Lastly, it contains laboratory results from a patient's hospitalization, detailing various blood parameters, and outlines the analytical parameters necessary for monitoring patients receiving parenteral nutrition.F√ìRMULA ESPEC√çFICA PARA DIABETES:A specialized formula designed for diabetic patients to help manage their blood glucose levels effectively.NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL SUPLEMENTARIA:Enteral nutrition is a method of delivering nutrients directly into the gastrointestinal tract, used in this case to support the patient's dietary needs.FIGURA 1:Figura 1 is a visual representation that compares the measured Gastroesophageal Reflux (GER) by Clinical Investigation (CI) with estimates derived from various predictive equations.MELAS SYNDROME:MELAS syndrome is a mitochondrial disorder characterized by mitochondrial encephalopathy, lactic acidosis, and stroke-like episodes, often leading to complications such as diabetes and other health issues.THORBURN DR:Thorburn DR is an author who has contributed to the understanding of mitochondrial disorders, including MELAS syndrome.RAHMAN S:Rahman S is a researcher who has studied genetic mutations related to mitochondrial disorders and their implications.PIA S:Pia S is an author who has written about MELAS syndrome and its clinical implications.NIEDERMAYR K:Niedermayr K is a researcher who has explored mitochondrial DNA mutations and their clinical presentations.IKEDA T:Ikeda T is a researcher who has studied mitochondrial DNA mutations in patients with MELAS syndrome.WANG XL:Wang XL is a researcher who has investigated the relationship between genetic polymorphisms and coronary artery disease.VOS MH:Vos MH is a researcher who has studied the dynamics of nitric oxide in mitochondrial function.KOGA Y:Koga Y is a researcher who has examined endothelial dysfunction in MELAS syndrome and its treatment options.NAINI A:Naini A is a researcher who has studied hypocitrullinemia in patients with MELAS syndrome.EL-HATTAB AW:El-Hattab AW is a researcher who has explored clinical manifestations and treatment options for MELAS syndrome.AL-GADI IS:Al-Gadi IS is a researcher who has studied endocrine disorders in primary mitochondrial disease.WHITTAKER RG:Whittaker RG is a researcher who has investigated the prevalence and progression of diabetes in mitochondrial disease.SCHAEFER AM:Schaefer AM is a researcher who has studied endocrine disorders in mitochondrial disease.SPROULE DM:Sproule DM is a researcher who has written about the clinical phenotype and management of MELAS syndrome.NESBITT V:Nesbitt V is a researcher involved in the UK MRC Mitochondrial Disease Patient Cohort Study.DE LAAT P:De Laat P is a researcher who has studied dysphagia and gastrointestinal problems in patients with mitochondrial disease.GENETIC MUTATION:Genetic mutations are alterations in the DNA sequence that can lead to various disorders, including those affecting mitochondrial function, such as MELAS syndrome.ENDOCRINE DISORDERS:Endocrine disorders are conditions that affect the endocrine system, which can be associated with mitochondrial diseases like MELAS syndrome.LACTIC ACIDOSIS:Lactic acidosis is a condition characterized by the buildup of lactic acid in the body, often seen in mitochondrial disorders like MELAS syndrome.STROKE-LIKE EPISODES:Stroke-like episodes are neurological events that can occur in patients with MELAS syndrome, leading to various symptoms and complications.MITOCHONDRIAL DNA:Mitochondrial DNA is the DNA located in mitochondria, which can harbor mutations leading to mitochondrial diseases, including MELAS syndrome.CLINICAL PHENOTYPE:Clinical phenotype refers to the observable characteristics and symptoms of a disease, which in the case of MELAS syndrome can vary widely among patients.TREATMENT OPTIONS:Treatment options for MELAS syndrome may include dietary management, exercise, and specific therapies aimed at managing symptoms and complications.DYSFUNCTION:Dysfunction refers to impaired or abnormal functioning, which can occur in various systems of the body in patients with mitochondrial diseases like MELAS syndrome.MITOCHONDRIAL DISORDERS:MAR√çA JOS√â MOLINA PUERTA:Mar√≠a Jos√© Molina Puerta is a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition at Hospital Universitario Reina Sof√≠a in C√≥rdoba.HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO REINA SOF√çA:Hospital Universitario Reina Sof√≠a is a medical institution located in C√≥rdoba, Spain. It serves as a clinical management unit that specializes in endocrinology and nutrition. Additionally, the hospital is involved in the treatment of oncology patients, providing a range of healthcare services to meet the needs of its community.DEHISCENCIA DE SUTURA:La dehiscencia de sutura es una complicaci√≥n postquir√∫rgica que puede ocurrir en pacientes que han sido sometidos a cirug√≠a, como la gastrectom√≠a. Esta condici√≥n se presenta cuando una herida quir√∫rgica se reabre despu√©s de la operaci√≥n, lo que puede llevar a complicaciones adicionales en el proceso de recuperaci√≥n del paciente.NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL TOTAL:NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL TOTAL, also known as total enteral nutrition, is a method of feeding that delivers all nutritional requirements directly through the gastrointestinal tract. This approach was specifically implemented for a patient following bariatric surgery due to complications that arose during the recovery process.MUJER DE 48 A√ëOS:Una mujer de 48 a√±os que ha sido intervenida de cirug√≠a bari√°trica y tiene antecedentes de diabetes tipo 2 y obesidad m√≥rbida.ENDOCOPIA DIGESTIVA ALTA:Procedimiento m√©dico que fue muy mal tolerado por la paciente durante la evaluaci√≥n de su condici√≥n.HIPERLIPEMIA:HIPERLIPEMIA is a medical condition characterized by elevated levels of lipids in the blood. This condition is significant as it can increase the risk of cardiovascular diseases. Additionally, hyperlipemia may also be a symptom associated with certain types of glucogenosis. The patient mentioned in the context has a history of this condition, indicating its relevance to her personal medical background.S√çNDROME DEPRESIVO:Condici√≥n m√©dica de la paciente que se menciona como parte de sus antecedentes personales.S√çNDROME DE APNEA OBSTRUCTIVA DEL SUE√ëO:Condici√≥n m√©dica de la paciente que est√° pendiente de confirmaci√≥n mediante estudio.MEFORMINA:Medicamento utilizado por la paciente para el control de la diabetes tipo 2.LOSART√ÅN:Medicamento utilizado por la paciente para el tratamiento de la hipertensi√≥n arterial.ATORVASTATINA:Atorvastatin is a medication that the patient takes to manage dyslipidemia and lower cholesterol levels. It is utilized by the patient for the control of blood lipids, indicating its role in maintaining healthy cholesterol levels and overall cardiovascular health.EZETROL:Medicamento utilizado por la paciente para el control de l√≠pidos en sangre.ADIRO:Medicamento utilizado por la paciente como parte de su tratamiento habitual.HIIPERTENSI√ìN ARTERIAL:ENDOSCOPIA DIGESTIVA ALTA:La endoscopia digestiva alta es un procedimiento realizado para evaluar el estado del es√≥fago, est√≥mago y duodeno de la paciente, que mostr√≥ una hernia de hiato y mucosa normal.ENFERMER√çA EDUCADORA:La enfermera educadora es responsable de la educaci√≥n nutricional de la paciente antes de la cirug√≠a bari√°trica.TOMOGRAF√çA AXIAL COMPUTERIZADA:La tomograf√≠a axial computerizada fue realizada para confirmar la existencia de un trayecto fistuloso en la sutura gastroyeyunal tras la cirug√≠a.DIETA DIAB√âTICA HIPERPROTEICA:La dieta diab√©tica hiperproteica es el r√©gimen alimenticio que se implement√≥ para la paciente durante su recuperaci√≥n postoperatoria.F√çSTULA G√ÅSTRICA:La f√≠stula g√°strica es una complicaci√≥n postoperatoria que se sospech√≥ en la paciente tras la cirug√≠a bari√°trica.DRENAJE:DRENAJE refers to the process of removing excess fluid from the body, which can include lymphatic fluid in conditions such as chylothorax or chylous ascites, in order to alleviate symptoms. In a specific case, drainage was performed on a patient to manage the dirty output following bariatric surgery. This procedure is essential for addressing complications that may arise postoperatively, ensuring the patient's recovery and comfort.FEDERACI√ìN INTERNACIONAL DE DIABETES:The Federaci√≥n Internacional de Diabetes (IDF) is an organization dedicated to establishing guidelines and recommendations for the treatment of diabetes. It plays a crucial role in providing guidance on various aspects of diabetes management, including the consideration of metabolic surgery for patients suffering from morbid obesity. Through its efforts, the IDF aims to improve the care and outcomes for individuals affected by diabetes worldwide.CONTROL GLUC√âMICO:Control Gluc√©mico is the process of managing blood glucose levels in individuals with diabetes, which is a crucial aspect of their treatment. This management involves dietary adjustments and the administration of insulin to effectively regulate blood glucose levels. Throughout the treatment, maintaining appropriate control gluc√©mico is essential for the patient's health, ensuring that their blood glucose levels remain stable. In particular, it has been noted that the control gluc√©mico was adequate during treatment with NET (Nutritional Enteral Therapy) and NPT (Nutritional Parenteral Therapy), highlighting the importance of continuous monitoring and management of glucose levels in diabetic patients.TRATAMIENTO M√âDICO INTENSIVO:El tratamiento m√©dico intensivo se refiere a las intervenciones m√©dicas que se aplican a la paciente para el control de la diabetes tipo 2, complementando la cirug√≠a bari√°trica.ESTUDIO STAMPEDE:El estudio STAMPEDE es una investigaci√≥n que demuestra la efectividad de la cirug√≠a bari√°trica en el control gluc√©mico en pacientes obesos con diabetes tipo 2.ASOCIACI√ìN AMERICANA DE DIABETES:The Asociaci√≥n Americana de Diabetes (ADA) provides recommendations for diabetes management, including the possibility of surgery for certain patients.SOCIEDAD ESPA√ëOLA DE LA CIRUG√çA DE LA ENFERMEDAD M√ìRBIDA:The Sociedad Espa√±ola de la Cirug√≠a de la Enfermedad M√≥rbida is a society that collaborates with SEEDO to establish guidelines for metabolic surgery.KUMPF:Kumpf is a researcher whose work reflects the outcomes of patients after metabolic surgery, particularly regarding dietary changes and complications.DOBRATZ:Dobratz is a researcher who evaluated predictive equations for measuring basal metabolic expenditure in obese women.CIRUG√çA METAB√ìLICA:Metabolic surgery is a medical procedure recommended for treating type 2 diabetes in patients with obesity.FUGA ANASTOM√ìTICA:Fuga anastom√≥tica is a common complication after metabolic surgery that may require nutritional support due to its impact on oral intake.NA√öSEAS Y V√ìMITOS:Nauseas y v√≥mitos are common post-operative complications that can limit a patient's ability to eat normally after metabolic surgery.MALABSORCI√ìN SEVERA:Malabsorci√≥n severa is a serious condition that can occur post-surgery, affecting nutrient absorption and necessitating nutritional support.EQUACIONES PREDICTIVAS:Ecuaciones predictivas are formulas used to estimate metabolic expenditure in patients, especially when calorimetry is unavailable.)<|COMPLETE|>SOCIEDAD AMERICANA DE NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL Y ENTERAL:The "SOCIEDAD AMERICANA DE NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL Y ENTERAL" (ASPEN) is an organization dedicated to advancing the field of nutritional support. It provides clinical guidelines for artificial nutritional support specifically for patients undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation. Additionally, ASPEN offers guidelines for nutritional support tailored to hospitalized adult obese patients, with a particular focus on those undergoing bariatric surgery. Through these initiatives, ASPEN aims to enhance the quality of care and outcomes for patients requiring specialized nutritional interventions.UNIDAD DE CUIDADOS INTENSIVOS:The Intensive Care Unit (UCI) is a specialized department in hospitals where patients receive close monitoring and care, particularly for nutritional adjustments.NIVEL DE RECOMENDACI√ìN BAJO:A low level of recommendation indicates that the evidence supporting a particular nutritional approach is not strong, as seen in the guidelines for nutritional support in bariatric surgery.NIVELES DE CUIDADO:Levels of care refer to the different intensities of medical care provided, particularly in the context of monitoring nutritional intake in critical patients.PAR√ÅMETROS CL√çNICOS:Clinical parameters are metrics used to assess a patient's health status, including wound healing and early mobilization in the context of nutritional support.DEFICIENCIAS NUTRICIONALES:Nutritional deficiencies are conditions that arise when the body does not get enough nutrients, which can be a risk for patients undergoing bariatric surgery.NIVELES DE GLUCEMIA:Blood glucose levels are monitored to manage the metabolic state of patients, particularly those undergoing bariatric surgery.)<|COMPLETE|>HOSPITAL GENERAL UNIVERSITARIO MORALES MESEGUER:Hospital General Universitario Morales Meseguer is a healthcare institution located in Murcia, specializing in various medical fields including endocrinology and nutrition.YAMINA SULEIMAN MARTOS:Dra. Yamina Suleiman Martos is a specialist in endocrinology and nutrition, involved in the clinical management of diabetes and related conditions.MURCIA:Murcia is an autonomous community in Spain known for its diverse healthcare and educational initiatives. It is home to the Hospital General Universitario Morales Meseguer, which serves the local population's medical needs. In 2003, Murcia hosted the National Congress on Pediatric Nutrition, highlighting its commitment to addressing nutritional issues within the community. Additionally, in this region, patients are required to submit a justification report to continue receiving treatment for their nutritional needs, reflecting the structured approach to healthcare management in Murcia.MNGIE:MNGIE, or mitochondrial neurogastrointestinal encephalomyopathy, is a rare genetic disorder characterized by a deficiency in thymidine phosphorylase, which is caused by mutations in the TYMP gene. This disorder primarily affects the gastrointestinal and nervous systems, resulting in a range of metabolic issues and complications, including diabetes. Due to the complexity of the symptoms and the interplay between the affected systems, MNGIE requires intricate treatment approaches to manage its severe manifestations.NUTRICIONAL SUPPLEMENTS:Nutritional supplements are products taken to enhance dietary intake, often used in clinical settings to support patients with specific health conditions.SUPLEMENTOS VITAM√çNICOS:Suplementos vitam√≠nicos are dietary products designed to provide essential vitamins that may be lacking in an individual's diet. These supplements are particularly beneficial for cancer patients, as they help to address specific vitamin deficiencies that may arise due to their health condition. Overall, vitamin supplements serve as a valuable resource for individuals seeking to enhance their nutritional intake and support their health.NEUROPAT√çA √ìPTICA BILATERAL:La neuropat√≠a √≥ptica bilateral es un diagn√≥stico que se presenta a los 10-12 a√±os, caracterizado por la disminuci√≥n progresiva de la agudeza visual y la atrofia √≥ptica observada en el fondo de ojo.POLINEUROPAT√çA SENSITIVO MOTORA AXONAL:La polineuropat√≠a sensitivo motora axonal es un diagn√≥stico que se presenta a los 16 a√±os, caracterizado por debilidad progresiva en miembros y amiotrofia distal.HIPOACUSIA PROFUNDA BILATERAL:La hipoacusia profunda bilateral neurosensorial es un diagn√≥stico que se presenta a los 30 a√±os, que requiere la colocaci√≥n de un implante coclear debido a la severidad de la p√©rdida auditiva.LEUCOENCEFALOPAT√çA SUPRATENTORIAL:La leucoencefalopat√≠a supratentorial periventricular difusa es un hallazgo observado en la resonancia magn√©tica nuclear cerebral, indicando cambios en la sustancia blanca del cerebro.S√çNTOMAS DIGESTIVOS:Los s√≠ntomas digestivos incluyen p√©rdida de peso, deposiciones blandas y episodios diarreicos, que se presentan en el paciente a lo largo del tiempo.AN√ÅLISIS DE SANGRE:The entity "AN√ÅLISIS DE SANGRE" refers to blood tests conducted on patients to assess various health indicators. These tests are essential for monitoring glucose levels, renal function, and other critical health parameters. The blood analysis reveals several important metrics, including blood sugar (glucemia), cholesterol levels, and vitamin concentrations, all of which are relevant for diagnosing the patient's health status. Overall, blood tests serve as a vital tool in evaluating the patient's overall health and nutritional parameters.C√ÅPSULA ENDOSC√ìPICA:La c√°psula endosc√≥pica visualiza linfangiectasias y edema en el yeyuno proximal, proporcionando informaci√≥n adicional sobre la condici√≥n del paciente.ATROFIA √ìPTICA:La atrofia √≥ptica es un hallazgo observado en el fondo de ojo del paciente, indicando da√±o severo en la v√≠a visual.DEBILIDAD PROGRESIVA:La debilidad progresiva es un s√≠ntoma que se presenta a los 16 a√±os, afectando principalmente a los miembros inferiores y superiores.HIPOGLICEMIAS:Las hipoglucemias son episodios que ocurren debido a la suspensi√≥n irregular del tratamiento antidiab√©tico, afectando la salud del paciente.SENSACI√ìN DE PLENITUD POSTPRANDIAL:La sensaci√≥n de plenitud postprandial es un s√≠ntoma que el paciente experimenta despu√©s de las comidas, indicando problemas digestivos.IMAGEN DE RMN:La imagen de resonancia magn√©tica nuclear (RMN) muestra cambios en la sustancia blanca del cerebro, lo que es relevante para el diagn√≥stico del paciente.PATOLOG√çAS:PATOLOG√çAS refer to diseases or disorders that can impact carbohydrate digestion and absorption. These health conditions necessitate nutritional support and dietary management, especially concerning FODMAP intake.TYMP:The TYMP gene is located on chromosome 22q13.33 and encodes a protein involved in the phosphorylation of thymidine, mutations in which cause MNGIE.GARONE:Garone is a researcher who reviewed 102 cases of MNGIE between 1988 and 2011, contributing to the understanding of the syndrome.TIMIDINA:Thymidine is a nucleoside that accumulates in patients with MNGIE due to the deficiency of thymidine phosphorylase.DEOXIURIDINA:Deoxyuridine is another nucleoside that accumulates in patients with MNGIE, indicating a metabolic disturbance.NEUROLOGICAL SYMPTOMS:Neurological symptoms associated with MNGIE include peripheral neuropathy, ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, and leukoencephalopathy.DIGESTIVE SYMPTOMS:Digestive symptoms of MNGIE include gastrointestinal dysmotility, early satiety, nausea, vomiting, and abdominal pain.ANALYTICAL FINDINGS:Analytical findings in MNGIE include elevated levels of thymidine and deoxyuridine in plasma, which are characteristic of the disorder.NEUROIMAGING FINDINGS:Neuroimaging findings in MNGIE typically show diffuse alterations in white matter, indicative of leukoencephalopathy.GENETIC SEQUENCING:Genetic sequencing is performed to identify mutations in the TYMP gene, confirming the diagnosis of MNGIE.CHROMOSOME 22Q13.33:COENZYME Q 10:Coenzyme Q 10 is an antioxidant and a vital component of the mitochondrial respiratory chain, evaluated for its effects in mitochondrial diseases.CREATINE:Creatine is a substance known for its ability to enhance muscle strength and performance, especially when used in conjunction with regular strength training. It has been the subject of research for its potential benefits in improving muscle strength in patients with mitochondrial diseases. Additionally, creatine has been studied for its effects on exercise tolerance in individuals with metabolic disorders, such as Glycogen Storage Disease Type VI (GSDVI), although the results of these studies have been mixed. Overall, creatine is recognized for its role in supporting muscle function and performance across various contexts.THYMIDINE PHOSPHORYLASE:Thymidine phosphorylase is an enzyme whose activity is crucial for the metabolism of thymidine, and its deficiency is linked to MNGIE.HEMATOPOIETIC STEM CELL TRANSPLANT:Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplant (HSCT) is a medical procedure that involves the transplantation of hematopoietic stem cells to treat a variety of diseases, particularly hematological malignancies and solid tumors. Additionally, HSCT is being investigated as a potential treatment option to address the enzymatic deficiency associated with Mitochondrial Neurogastrointestinal Encephalomyopathy (MNGIE). This procedure represents a significant therapeutic approach in the field of regenerative medicine and offers hope for patients with challenging medical conditions.DIETARY ANTIOXIDANTS:Dietary antioxidants, including idebenone and vitamin E, have been studied for their potential clinical benefits in mitochondrial diseases.S√çNDROME DE COLON IRRITABLE:El s√≠ndrome de colon irritable es un trastorno gastrointestinal que se caracteriza por s√≠ntomas como dolor abdominal y cambios en los h√°bitos intestinales. Este trastorno puede estar relacionado con la sensibilidad al gluten. Adem√°s, es importante diferenciar el s√≠ndrome de colon irritable de enfermedades mitocondriales, ya que aunque ambos pueden presentar s√≠ntomas gastrointestinales, son condiciones distintas.DISTROFIA MUSCULAR CONG√âNITA:Distrofia muscular cong√©nita por d√©ficit de merosina is a genetic disorder that affects muscle function and is relevant in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases.LEUCODISTROFIAS:Leucodistrofias are a group of disorders affecting the white matter of the brain, included in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases.ENFERMEDAD DE KRABBE:Enfermedad de Krabbe is a type of leukodystrophy that must be differentiated from mitochondrial diseases.ENFERMEDAD DE CANAVAN:Enfermedad de Canavan is a genetic disorder that affects the brain and is included in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases.ENFERMEDAD DE ALEXANDER:Enfermedad de Alexander is a type of leukodystrophy that must be differentiated from mitochondrial diseases.LEUCOENCEFALOPAT√çA CON SUSTANCIA BLANCA EVANESCENTE:Leucoencefalopat√≠a con sustancia blanca evanescente is a disorder affecting the brain's white matter, relevant in the differential diagnosis of mitochondrial diseases.POLINEUROPAT√çA DESMIELINIZANTE INFLAMATORIA CR√ìNICA:TIMIDIN FOSFORILASA:Thymidine phosphorylase is an enzyme whose activity is crucial for correcting metabolic damage in patients with MNGIE.HEPATITIS:Hepatitis is a medical condition characterized by inflammation of the liver, which can be caused by various factors including viral infections, alcohol use, or autoimmune diseases. It is important to note that hepatitis can also manifest as a rare complication in the early weeks of treatment with the ketogenic diet. In the context of specific medical conditions, hepatic transplantation is considered a viable treatment option for patients with Mitochondrial Neurogastrointestinal Encephalomyopathy (MNGIE), due to the liver's essential role in producing thymidine phosphorylase.DIABETES MITOCONDRIAL:Mitochondrial diabetes is a condition that complicates treatment in patients with MNGIE, requiring careful management of blood sugar levels.COENZIMA Q 10:Coenzyme Q10 is a supplement used in the treatment of mitochondrial disorders, including MNGIE, to support cellular energy production.L-CARNITINA:L-CARNITINA, also known as L-carnitine, is an amino acid that plays a crucial role in energy metabolism. It is often included in infant formulas to support the energy needs of infants. Additionally, L-carnitine is utilized as a dietary supplement to assist in managing symptoms associated with mitochondrial diseases, such as MNGIE. In cancer patients, L-carnitine may help increase body weight and enhance overall quality of life. Furthermore, it is recognized for its benefits in optimizing weight and body composition among athletes. L-carnitine is also important in the treatment of Œ≤-oxidation disorders, as it aids in the transport of fatty acids into the mitochondria for energy production.ANTIDIAB√âTICOS ORALES:Oral antidiabetic medications are prescribed to manage the patient's diabetes, which is complicated by mitochondrial dysfunction.REPAGLINIDA:Repaglinide is another oral antidiabetic medication prescribed to the patient to help control blood sugar levels.AMAUROSIS:Amaurosis is a condition affecting the patient, complicating their ability to self-administer insulin.CUIDADORA PRINCIPAL:The primary caregiver, who is the patient's mother, assists in managing the patient's treatment and understanding insulin dosage adjustments.TRASPLANTE DE C√âLULAS HEMATOPI√âTICAS:Hematopoietic cell transplantation, also known as stem cell transplantation, is a medical procedure designed to restore blood cell production in patients suffering from specific diseases. This treatment involves the transplantation of hematopoietic stem cells, which are crucial for generating blood cells. Although hematopoietic stem cell transplantation was considered as a treatment option for the patient, it was ultimately rejected.MERCEDES V√ÅZQUEZ GUTI√âRREZ:Dr. Mercedes V√°zquez Guti√©rrez is a specialist in endocrinology and nutrition at the Hospital Universitario de Torrec√°rdenas in Almer√≠a. She is actively involved in providing nutritional support for renal patients, demonstrating her expertise in both endocrinology and nutrition within the healthcare setting.HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO DE TORREC√ÅRDENAS:Hospital Universitario de Torrec√°rdenas is a clinical unit specializing in Endocrinology and Nutrition located in Almer√≠a.UCI:The Unidad de Cuidados Intensivos (UCI), also known as the Intensive Care Unit, is a specialized area within a hospital dedicated to providing intensive medical care to critically ill patients. This unit is equipped to handle a variety of severe medical conditions, including but not limited to severe pancreatitis and acute renal failure. Patients in the UCI receive close monitoring and specialized treatment tailored to their critical health needs. The UCI plays a crucial role in managing complications and ensuring that patients in critical condition receive the necessary care to stabilize their health.DUODENOPANCREATECTOM√çA SUBTOTAL:Subtotal duodenopancreatectomy is the surgical procedure performed on the patient to address the pancreatic tumor.BIOPSIA:BIOPSIA refers to a medical procedure involving the extraction of tissue for diagnostic purposes. In one instance, a biopsy was performed via endoscopic ultrasound to confirm the diagnosis of a pancreatic mucinous tumor. Additionally, the term encompasses intestinal biopsy, which is a diagnostic procedure that involves the removal of tissue from the small intestine to assess damage caused by celiac disease. Thus, BIOPSIA encompasses various types of biopsies used to diagnose different conditions, including pancreatic tumors and intestinal disorders.SANGRADO MASIVO:Massive bleeding occurred post-surgery, necessitating urgent intervention for the patient.DEHISCENCIA DE LA ANASTOMOSIS PANCREATOYEYUNAL:Dehiscence of the pancreatojejunostomy occurred, leading to further complications for the patient.PANCREATITIS AGUDA NECR√ìTICO HEMORR√ÅGICA:Acute necrotizing hemorrhagic pancreatitis developed in the patient, complicating their recovery.VAR√ìN:VAR√ìN is a 75-year-old male individual who has been admitted due to an ischemic stroke. He weighs 70 kg and has a height of 1.67 m. His energy expenditure is calculated using specific formulas, and he requires enteral nutrition to support his recovery.F√çSTULA PANCRE√ÅTICA:F√çSTULA PANCRE√ÅTICA is a medical complication that can arise following pancreatic surgery or as a result of pancreatitis. It is characterized by an abnormal connection between the pancreas and other organs, which leads to the leakage of pancreatic fluids. This condition can manifest as an external fistula with low to moderate output. The formation of a fistula is a pathological condition that poses significant health risks and requires careful management.EQUIPO M√âDICO:El "EQUIPO M√âDICO" es un grupo de profesionales de la salud encargado de la supervisi√≥n y administraci√≥n de la nutrici√≥n parenteral, as√≠ como de la evaluaci√≥n del estado nutricional del paciente. Este equipo desempe√±a un papel crucial en el tratamiento y la recuperaci√≥n del paciente, asegurando que se realicen diagn√≥sticos y tratamientos adecuados para condiciones espec√≠ficas como el quilot√≥rax y la ascitis quilosa. En resumen, el equipo m√©dico no solo se enfoca en la nutrici√≥n, sino que tambi√©n abarca un enfoque integral en la atenci√≥n y el tratamiento del paciente durante su proceso de recuperaci√≥n.S√çNTOMAS CL√çNICOS:Los s√≠ntomas cl√≠nicos incluyen hiperglucemia, esteatorrea y p√©rdida de peso, que el paciente ha estado experimentando durante su tratamiento.DIFICULTAD PARA AUMENTAR PESO:El paciente ha reportado dificultad para aumentar de peso a pesar de seguir las indicaciones diet√©ticas y terap√©uticas.F√ìRMULA POLIM√âRICA NORMOCAL√ìRICA:La f√≥rmula polim√©rica normocal√≥rica es un suplemento nutricional que se prescribe al paciente para ayudar a cubrir sus requerimientos energ√©ticos.F√ìRMULA HIPERCAL√ìRICA ESPEC√çFICA DE DIABETES:La f√≥rmula hipercal√≥rica espec√≠fica de diabetes es un tipo de suplemento que se utiliza para ayudar al paciente a aumentar de peso mientras controla su glucemia.DESNUTRICI√ìN SEVERA:ENZYMES PANCRE√ÅTICAS:Enzymes pancre√°ticas are supplements used to aid digestion in patients with pancreatic insufficiency, particularly in those with diabetes.F√ìRMULAS NUTRICIONALES ESPEC√çFICAS:F√≥rmulas nutricionales espec√≠ficas are specialized dietary formulas designed to meet the nutritional needs of diabetic patients.CIRUG√çA DIGESTIVA:CIRUG√çA DIGESTIVA refers to surgical procedures involving the digestive tract, which require careful nutritional management postoperatively.MULTIDISCIPLINAR:Multidisciplinar refers to the collaborative approach involving various healthcare professionals to manage complex medical conditions like diabetes.HORMONAS REGULADORAS:Hormonas reguladoras are hormones that regulate carbohydrate metabolism, including insulin and glucagon, which are affected by pancreatic surgery.PANCREATIC RESECTION:GARIN GROUP:The GARIN group is the Andalusian Group for Nutrition Reflection and Investigation, which focuses on managing patients with diabetes and stress hyperglycemia.PANCREATICODUODENECTOMY:Pancreaticoduodenectomy, commonly referred to as the Whipple procedure, is a complex surgical operation that involves the removal of the head of the pancreas, part of the small intestine (duodenum), and other nearby structures. This procedure is primarily performed on patients diagnosed with pancreatic cancer. Due to the extensive nature of the surgery, patients often require specialized nutritional support during their recovery.NUTRITION SUPPORT:Nutrition support encompasses a range of methods designed to provide nutritional intake to patients who are unable to eat normally due to various medical conditions. This includes enteral feeding, which delivers nutrients directly to the gastrointestinal tract, and parenteral feeding, which provides nutrients intravenously. It is particularly crucial for managing conditions such as acute pancreatitis, where patients may struggle to absorb food effectively. 

Nutrition support is essential in critical care settings and is especially important for hospitalized pediatric patients, including those with obesity or other health issues. The primary goal of nutrition support is to ensure that patients receive adequate nutrients to meet their needs when they cannot do so through regular eating. Overall, nutrition support plays a vital role in the recovery and management of patients with significant health challenges.TAPIA MJ:Tapia MJ is a researcher and author known for their contributions to the field of diabetes management, particularly in relation to parenteral nutrition. They have co-authored studies that examine the prevalence of diabetes and its management within the context of parenteral nutrition. Additionally, Tapia MJ played a significant role in a study focused on parenteral nutrition-associated hyperglycemia, which was published in the journal Diabetes Care. Their work highlights the critical intersection of diabetes care and nutritional support in clinical settings.OC√ìN J:Oc√≥n J is a contributor to research on insulin therapy and metabolic control in patients on total parenteral nutrition.CABREJAS-G√ìMEZ C:Cabrejas-G√≥mez C is part of the study group focusing on hyperglycemia in parenteral nutrition.BALLENTEROS-POMAR MD:Ballenteros-Pomar MD is a researcher involved in the study of diabetes management in parenteral nutrition.GONZALEZ INFANTINO CA:Gonzalez Infantino CA is an author who studied hyperglycemia and hypoalbuminemia as mortality factors in patients with enteral feeding.GONZ√ÅLEZ CD:Gonz√°lez CD is a researcher who contributed to studies on hyperglycemia in patients receiving enteral nutrition.SANCHEZ R:Sanchez R is an author involved in research on hyperglycemia and its effects on patients with enteral feeding.PRESNER N:Presner N is a researcher who studied the impact of hyperglycemia in enteral feeding patients.LASSEN K:Lassen K is a notable contributor in the field of perioperative care, particularly in colorectal surgery and pancreaticoduodenectomy. They have played a significant role in the consensus review of optimal perioperative care practices for colorectal surgery. Additionally, Lassen K has contributed to the development of guidelines for perioperative care in pancreaticoduodenectomy, specifically as an author for the guidelines established by the ERAS Society. Their research and contributions are instrumental in enhancing the standards of care in these surgical areas.COOLSEN MM:Coolsen MM is involved in research comparing feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.SLIM K:Slim K is a researcher who contributed to guidelines for the care of patients undergoing pancreaticoduodenectomy.DE AGUILAR- NACIMIENTO JE:De Aguilar-Nacimiento JE is a contributor to research on postoperative care in pancreaticoduodenectomy.SCH√ÑFER M:Sch√§fer M is a researcher and author who has made significant contributions to the field of medicine. They have played a key role in developing guidelines for managing patients after pancreaticoduodenectomy, a complex surgical procedure. Additionally, Sch√§fer M has conducted studies on immunonutrition, exploring its implications and benefits in patient care. Their work reflects a commitment to improving outcomes for patients through evidence-based practices and innovative nutritional strategies.GUILBAUD T:Guilbaud T is a researcher who compared different feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.BIRNBAUM DJ:Birnbaum DJ is involved in research on feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.LOUBI√àRE S:Loub√®re S is a researcher who contributed to studies on feeding regimes post-surgery.BONNET J:Bonnet J is a researcher involved in the analysis of feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.CHOPINET S:Chopinet S is a researcher who contributed to studies on nutritional support after pancreaticoduodenectomy.GR√âGOIRE E:Gr√©goire E is involved in research comparing feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.BERDAH S:Berdah S is a researcher who contributed to studies on nutritional support in surgical patients.HARDWIGSEN J:Hardwigsen J is involved in research on postoperative care and nutrition.MOUTARDIER V:Moutardier V is a researcher who contributed to studies on feeding regimes after pancreaticoduodenectomy.DAS BC:Das BC is a researcher who studied the effects of enteral feeding timing in post-pancreaticoduodenectomy patients.HAQUE M:Haque M is involved in research on enteral feeding in patients after pancreaticoduodenectomy.UDDIN MS:Uddin MS is a researcher who studied the effects of enteral feeding in post-surgical patients.NUR-E-ELAHI M:Nur-E-Elahi M is involved in research on enteral feeding in patients with diabetes.KHAN ZR:Khan ZR is a researcher who studied the effects of enteral feeding in diabetic patients.MC MAHON MM:McMahon MM is a contributor to clinical guidelines for nutrition support in hyperglycemic patients.NYSTROM E:Nystrom E is actively engaged in the development of clinical guidelines for nutrition support, contributing to the establishment of best practices in this field. Additionally, Nystrom E is one of the authors who discusses treatment outcomes related to early enteral versus early parenteral nutrition, highlighting the importance of nutritional strategies in clinical settings.BRAUNSCHWEIG C:Braunschweig C is a researcher who has made significant contributions to the field of clinical guidelines, particularly in the management of hyperglycemia. Additionally, Braunschweig C is one of the authors involved in developing guidelines for nutrition support therapy in critically ill patients. Furthermore, Braunschweig C has also contributed to discussions regarding treatment outcomes comparing early enteral nutrition to early parenteral nutrition. Through these efforts, Braunschweig C has played a vital role in advancing knowledge and practices related to nutrition and metabolic management in clinical settings.MILES J:Miles J is actively engaged in the development of clinical guidelines for nutrition support specifically tailored for patients with diabetes. Additionally, Miles J is one of the authors who contribute to the discussion on treatment outcomes comparing early enteral nutrition to early parenteral nutrition. This involvement highlights Miles J's commitment to advancing nutritional practices and improving patient care in the context of diabetes management.ALEJANDRO SANZ-PAR√çS:Alejandro Sanz-Par√≠s is a researcher involved in studies on enteral nutrition formulas for diabetes management.DIANA BOJ-CARCELLER:Diana Boj-Carceller is a researcher who contributed to studies on enteral nutrition for diabetic patients.BEATRIZ LARDIES-SANCHEZ:Beatriz Lardies-Sanchez is involved in research on enteral nutrition for diabetes management.LETICIA PEREZ-FERN√ÅNDEZ:Leticia Perez-Fern√°ndez is a researcher who contributed to studies on nutrition for diabetic patients.)<|COMPLETE|>DANIEL A. DE LUIS ROM√ÅN:Dr. Daniel A. de Luis Rom√°n is the scientific director and head of the Endocrinology and Nutrition Service at Hospital Cl√≠nico Universitario de Valladolid.UNIVERSIDAD DE VALLADOLID:The Universidad de Valladolid is a prominent higher education institution located in Spain, known for its comprehensive research and educational programs across various fields, particularly in medicine and nutrition. The university is recognized for its significant contributions to research in endocrinology and nutrition. Notably, Prof. Dr. Daniel de Luis Rom√°n serves as a professor of Endocrinology and Nutrition at this esteemed institution, further enhancing its reputation in these specialized areas of study.ANA ISABEL S√ÅNCHEZ MARCOS:Dra. Ana Isabel S√°nchez Marcos is a specialist in Endocrinology and Nutrition, focusing on eating disorders in patients with diabetes.TRASTORNO DEL COMPORTAMIENTO ALIMENTARIO:"TRASTORNO DEL COMPORTAMIENTO ALIMENTARIO," or Eating Disorders (TCA), refers to a group of psychiatric diseases that are marked by significant medical complications. These disorders are characterized by persistent disturbances in food intake, which can impair both health and psychosocial functioning. Additionally, this condition notably affects patients with diabetes, particularly young women diagnosed with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1).HYPERCALORIC DIABETES-SPECIFIC ENTERAL NUTRITIONAL FORMULA:A specialized nutritional formula designed for patients with diabetes to improve their nutritional status and glycemic control.DIABETES TECHNOLOGY:Diabetes technology refers to the tools and methods used to manage diabetes, including enteral formulas and monitoring devices.AMERICAN PSYCHIATRIC ASSOCIATION:The American Psychiatric Association (APA) is a professional organization dedicated to the advancement of mental health care. It provides comprehensive guidelines for the diagnosis and treatment of various mental disorders, including eating disorders such as anorexia nerviosa. The APA publishes essential resources, including the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-5), which outlines the criteria for diagnosing these conditions. Additionally, the organization offers specific recommendations regarding the nutritional care of patients suffering from anorexia nerviosa, ensuring that practitioners have the necessary tools to provide effective treatment.FUNDACI√ìN SED:The Fundaci√≥n SED is an organization in Spain that provides data and support for individuals with diabetes, including statistics on the prevalence of diabetes in minors.TRASTORNO DE EVITACI√ìN/RESTRICCI√ìN DE ALIMENTOS:Avoidant/Restrictive Food Intake Disorder is characterized by a lack of interest in eating or avoidance of certain foods, which can lead to nutritional deficiencies.TRASTORNO DE ATRACONES:Binge Eating Disorder involves recurrent episodes of eating large quantities of food, often to the point of discomfort, and is associated with feelings of loss of control.PICA:Pica is an eating disorder characterized by the persistent consumption of non-nutritive, non-food substances, which can lead to serious health complications.TRASTORNO DE RUMIACI√ìN:Rumination Disorder involves the repeated regurgitation of food, which may be re-chewed, re-swallowed, or spit out, and can have significant health implications.CIE-9:The International Classification of Diseases, 9th Revision (ICD-9) is a system used for coding and classifying diseases, including eating disorders.MUJER DM1:A female patient diagnosed with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM1) who has experienced poor metabolic control and has a history of eating disorders.ENDOCRINOLOG√çA PEDI√ÅTRICA:Pediatric endocrinology department that provided follow-up care for the patient transitioning to adult care.MADRE:MADRE refers to the concept of a mother, who plays a crucial role in the development of a newborn's microbiome through close contact during birth and early feeding. This relationship is essential for establishing a healthy microbial environment for the infant. Additionally, in a specific case, the mother of a patient has a history of thyroid crises and is a smoker, which may influence the patient's overall health. This highlights the multifaceted impact a mother can have, not only in nurturing and supporting the newborn's development but also in potentially affecting the health outcomes of older children or patients through her own health behaviors and medical history.ABUELA:The grandmother of the patient who has a diagnosis of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (DM2), indicating a family history of diabetes.TCA:TCA, or Trastornos de la Conducta Alimentaria, refers to a group of psychiatric disorders that encompass conditions such as anorexia nerviosa and other eating disorders characterized by abnormal eating behaviors and attitudes towards food and weight control. Patients struggling with TCA often exhibit restrictive eating and purging behaviors, which can significantly impact their nutritional intake and overall health. 

These eating disorders are particularly prevalent among individuals with diabetes mellitus type 1 (DM1), especially young women, and can exacerbate the complications associated with diabetes. The presence of TCA may necessitate dietary treatment and nutritional education to address the specific needs of affected patients. In some cases, the severity of the disorder may influence the need for involuntary treatment to ensure the patient's safety and well-being.CRISIS TIROT√ìXICAS:Thyroid crises experienced by the patient's mother, which may have health implications for the patient.MALA ADAPTACI√ìN SOCIAL ESCOLAR:The poor social adaptation of the patient in school, which may relate to her health and psychological state.INFUSORA SUBCUT√ÅNEA CONTINUA DE INSULINA:The continuous subcutaneous insulin infusion device used by the patient to manage her diabetes.PADRES:The entity "PADRES" refers to parents who hold the responsibility of ensuring safe nutrition for their children with food allergies.UTCA:UTCA, or Unidad de Trastornos de la Conducta Alimentaria, is a specialized healthcare facility dedicated to the treatment of eating disorders. It offers comprehensive care that addresses both nutritional and psychological issues faced by patients. The unit operates through a collaborative approach, integrating the expertise of various disciplines, including Psychiatry, Psychology, and Endocrinology, to provide holistic treatment tailored to the needs of individuals struggling with these disorders.ECG:Electrocardiogram (ECG) is a test performed to monitor the patient's heart activity and detect any abnormalities.ANAL√çTICA:ANAL√çTICA refers to the laboratory tests conducted to evaluate a patient's biochemical markers and overall health status. These analytical tests are essential for monitoring various blood parameters, including blood counts, enzyme levels, blood sugar levels, and nutritional deficiencies. By assessing these factors, ANAL√çTICA provides valuable insights into the patient's condition, helping healthcare professionals make informed decisions regarding diagnosis and treatment.TENSION ARTERIAL:Blood pressure measurement is taken to assess the cardiovascular health of the patient.FRECUENCIA CARDIACA:Heart rate measurement is conducted to monitor the patient's heart function and overall health.DMO:Bone Mineral Density (DMO) assessment is performed to evaluate the strength and health of the patient's bones.HOSPITAL DE D√çA:Hospital de D√≠a is a day hospital where the patient was admitted for treatment from May 2015 to July 2015.LORACEPAM:Lorazepam is a medication prescribed to the patient to help manage anxiety as part of their treatment plan.DIAGN√ìSTICO NUTRICIONAL:The nutritional diagnosis indicates the patient's condition, including normonutrition with a decrease in prealbumin and deficiencies in zinc and vitamin D3.TRATAMIENTO INICIAL:The initial treatment proposed for the patient includes dietary recommendations, insulin adjustments, and supplementation for deficiencies.RECUPERACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:Nutritional recovery involves achieving metabolic stability and addressing the patient's malnutrition.RECUPERACI√ìN PSIQUI√ÅTRICA:Psychiatric recovery focuses on stabilizing the patient's eating behaviors and addressing their restrictive conduct.CETONURIA:Cetonuria refers to the presence of ketones in the urine, which serves as an important indicator of metabolic processes in the body. This condition often signifies a lack of insulin, raising concerns about the potential for cetoacidosis, particularly in diabetic patients. Additionally, ketonuria highlights poor control of diabetes, suggesting that the patient's blood sugar levels are not being effectively managed. Overall, cetonuria is a critical marker for assessing the health and metabolic status of individuals with diabetes.REVISI√ìN TRIMESTRAL:The quarterly review is a follow-up appointment to assess the patient's progress and health status.HOSPITAL DE D√çA UTCA:Hospital where the patient was admitted for psychiatric recovery and stabilization of eating behaviors from May 2015 to July 2015MONITORIZACI√ìN FLASH DE GLUCOSA:A glucose monitoring system that improved the patient's glycemic controlJULIO 2015:The month when the patient was discharged from Hospital de D√≠a UTCA after stabilizationAGOSTO 2016:The month when the patient was discharged again from Hospital de D√≠a UTCA after a new admissionMULTIDOSIS:A method of insulin administration that the patient switched to after her initial treatmentDEGLUDEC:Another type of insulin that the patient was prescribed as part of her diabetes managementMAYO 2015:M. DELGADO G√ìMEZ:Dr. M. Delgado G√≥mez is one of the doctors acknowledged for sharing clinical follow-up of patients with diabetes and eating disorders.D. GONZ√ÅLEZ PARRA:Dr. D. Gonz√°lez Parra is another doctor acknowledged for sharing clinical follow-up of patients with diabetes and eating disorders.L. BARRERA AGUADO:Dr. L. Barrera Aguado is also acknowledged for sharing clinical follow-up of patients with diabetes and eating disorders.AUTOCUIDADO:Self-care refers to the practices that patients with Type 1 diabetes must adopt to manage their condition effectively, including monitoring blood glucose and administering insulin.HIPOGLUCEMIAS:HIPOGLUCEMIAS refer to episodes of low blood sugar that can occur in various medical conditions. Specifically, they are commonly observed in patients with glycogen storage diseases, particularly during the early years of life. Additionally, hypoglycemia can also occur in patients with diabetes when blood sugar levels fall too low, necessitating immediate intervention. This condition highlights the importance of monitoring blood sugar levels in affected individuals to prevent potential complications.ESTEFAN√çA SANTOS MAZO:Estefan√≠a Santos Mazo is a medical specialist in endocrinology and nutrition at the Hospital Universitario de Burgos.APACHE SCORE:The APACHE score is a severity scoring system used to measure the severity of disease in patients, particularly in critical care settings.RANSON CRITERIA:CIRUG√çA GENERAL:CIRUG√çA GENERAL is the medical department responsible for performing surgical procedures, specifically focusing on the evaluation and management of patients with suspected pancreatitis. In this context, the department is involved in the placement of enteral nutrition tubes, ensuring that patients receive the necessary nutritional support during their treatment.PANCREATITIS AGUDA EDEMATOSA:Pancreatitis aguda edematosa is the medical condition diagnosed in the patient, characterized by inflammation of the pancreas.VAR√ìN DE 56 A√ëOS:A 56-year-old male patient presenting with abdominal pain and a history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes.HEMANGIOMA CEREBELOSO:Hemangioma cerebeloso is a previous medical condition noted in the patient's history.TC ABDOMINAL:TC abdominal is the imaging study conducted to evaluate the patient's abdominal organs and diagnose pancreatitis.CLORURO M√ìRFICO:Cloruro m√≥rfico is the analgesic medication administered to manage the patient's pain.EPIGASTRALGIA:Epigastralgia refers to pain in the upper central region of the abdomen, which the patient experienced during feeding. This condition is characterized by severe abdominal pain that ultimately led to the patient's hospital admission.MURPHY:Murphy's sign is a clinical test used to assess for gallbladder inflammation, which was positive in the patient's examination.LEU 18.000:Leu 18.000 refers to the elevated white blood cell count found in the patient's laboratory tests, indicating possible infection or inflammation.TROPNINA ALTA SENSIBILIDAD:Troponina alta sensibilidad is a cardiac marker that was tested to rule out myocardial infarction in the patient.GLUCEMIA 290 MG/DL:Glucemia 290 mg/dL indicates the patient's elevated blood sugar level at the time of admission.DIGESTIVO:The DIGESTIVO service is dedicated to managing patients with gastrointestinal disorders, particularly those related to nutrition. This service is consulted for the endoscopic placement of enteral feeding tubes for patients, ensuring that individuals with specific dietary needs receive appropriate nutritional support.RANSON:RANSON refers to a scoring system utilized to evaluate the severity of acute pancreatitis in patients. This system comprises a set of criteria that incorporates both clinical and laboratory findings, allowing healthcare professionals to assess the condition's seriousness effectively. The Ranson criteria are essential for guiding treatment decisions and predicting patient outcomes in cases of acute pancreatitis.APACHE II:APACHE II is a severity scoring system designed to evaluate the prognosis of critically ill patients, including those suffering from acute pancreatitis. This scoring system measures the severity of illness in patients, providing a quantitative assessment that helps in determining the risk of complications. Specifically, a score of 10 or higher on the APACHE II scale indicates an increased risk of adverse outcomes. The tool is widely utilized in clinical settings to guide treatment decisions and improve patient management for those in critical condition.SONDA DE ALIMENTACI√ìN ENTERAL:La sonda de alimentaci√≥n enteral es un dispositivo que se planea colocar para proporcionar nutrici√≥n al paciente de manera directa al yeyuno.OBESIDAD GRADO 1:NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL TOTAL (NPT):NPT es un tipo de nutrici√≥n administrada intravenosamente que el paciente ha estado recibiendo para mantener su estado nutricional.SONDA NASOENTERAL:The "SONDA NASOENTERAL" refers to a medical procedure involving the use of a nasoenteral tube, which is a type of feeding tube designed for providing enteral nutrition to patients who are unable to eat by mouth. This device is particularly indicated for short-term use and is beneficial for patients with issues such as delayed gastric emptying or a risk of aspiration. 

The nasoenteral tube can be categorized into specific types, including the nasoduodenal (SND) and nasoyeyunal (SNY) tubes, which allow for the infusion of nutrition beyond the pylorus, directly into the stomach or intestines. While effective for delivering nutrition, the use of nasoenteral tubes can lead to complications and may cause agitation in patients with decreased consciousness. Overall, the SONDA NASOENTERAL serves as a critical tool in the management of patients requiring enteral feeding.TC (TOMOGRAF√çA COMPUTARIZADA):La TC es un procedimiento de imagen que se realiz√≥ para evaluar la evoluci√≥n del paciente con pancreatitis aguda.VANCOMICINA:Vancomicina is a broad-spectrum antibiotic that has been administered empirically to patients during their treatment. It has been shown to increase the abundance of Akkermansia muciniphila in rodents, indicating its potential effects on gut microbiota.HEMOCULTIVO:El hemocultivo es una prueba que se realiz√≥ para detectar infecciones en el paciente, mostrando crecimiento de cocos gram positivos.GLUCEMIAS:GLUCEMIAS se refiere a las mediciones de los niveles de az√∫car en la sangre. Estas mediciones son especialmente importantes en el contexto de pacientes con Alzheimer, ya que se utilizan para ajustar el tratamiento de la enfermedad.REPOSO PANCRE√ÅTICO:El reposo pancre√°tico es una estrategia terap√©utica que se ha utilizado en el tratamiento de la pancreatitis, aunque su eficacia ha sido cuestionada.FALLO ORG√ÅNICO:FALLO ORG√ÅNICO refers to a serious condition characterized by the failure of one or more organs, which may require the use of specialized nutritional formulas to support the affected individual. This condition is particularly critical in patients suffering from acute pancreatitis, where it can manifest as a severe complication. The presence of organ failure in these patients can extend beyond 48 hours and is associated with an increased mortality rate, especially in severe cases.LITIASIS BILIAR:LITIASIS BILIAR, also known as biliary lithiasis or gallstones, is a significant medical condition characterized by the formation of stones in the bile ducts. It is recognized as the most common cause of acute pancreatitis in various countries, accounting for approximately 35% of episodes. This condition can arise as a complication of obesity, which negatively impacts digestive health and necessitates appropriate management. In particular, biliary lithiasis is one of the most prevalent etiologies of acute pancreatitis, as the presence of gallstones can obstruct the pancreatic duct, leading to inflammation and other serious complications.CONSUMO EXCESIVO DE ALCOHOL:El consumo excesivo de alcohol es una de las principales causas de pancreatitis aguda, contribuyendo al da√±o del tejido pancre√°tico.HIDROELECTROL√çTICOS:Los desequilibrios hidroelectrol√≠ticos son complicaciones comunes en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda, afectando su tratamiento y recuperaci√≥n.NECROSIS DEL PAR√âNQUIMA:La necrosis del par√©nquima pancre√°tico puede ocurrir en casos severos de pancreatitis aguda, complicando el tratamiento y aumentando el riesgo de mortalidad.CRITERIOS DE RANSON:Los criterios de Ranson son un conjunto de par√°metros cl√≠nicos utilizados para predecir la gravedad y el pron√≥stico de la pancreatitis aguda.NECESIDAD DE CIRUG√çA:La necesidad de cirug√≠a puede surgir en pacientes con pancreatitis aguda debido a complicaciones, lo que puede influir en el tipo de soporte nutricional requerido.SOLUCI√ìN POLIM√âRICA:Soluci√≥n polim√©rica refers to a standard polymeric solution used for enteral nutrition in patients.DICKERSON RN:Dickerson RN is an author known for contributions to research in the field of nutrition and metabolism. Specifically, Dickerson RN has focused on studying resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis, providing valuable insights into the metabolic needs of this patient population. Additionally, Dickerson RN has conducted research on parenteral nutrition solutions, further enhancing the understanding of nutritional support for individuals unable to consume food orally.VEHE KL:Vehe KL is an author who collaborated on a study regarding resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.MULLEN JL:Mullen JL is an author involved in research on resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.FEURER ID:Feurer ID is an author who contributed to the study of resting energy expenditure in patients with pancreatitis.MARULENDRA S:Marulendra S is an author who worked on nutrition support in pancreatitis.KIRBY D:Kirby D is an author who co-authored a paper on nutrition support in pancreatitis.HAVALA T:Havala T is an author who researched nutritional support in acute pancreatitis.SHRONTS E:Shronts E is an author who contributed to the study of nutritional support in acute pancreatitis.CERRA F:Cerra F is an author involved in research on nutritional support in acute pancreatitis.KOV√ÅR J:Kov√°r J is an author who studied the effects of hyperglycemia on lipoprotein lipase activity.FEJFAROV√Å V:Fejfarov√° V is an author who collaborated on research regarding hyperglycemia and lipoprotein lipase activity.PELIK√ÅNOV√Å T:Pelik√°nov√° T is an author who contributed to the study of hyperglycemia's effects on lipoprotein lipase activity.POLEDNE R:Poledne R is an author involved in research on hyperglycemia and lipoprotein lipase activity.HELTON WS:Helton WS is an author who wrote about intravenous nutrition in patients with acute pancreatitis.ROMBEAU JL:Rombeau JL is an editor of a book on clinical nutrition, which encompasses the topic of parenteral nutrition.KOHN CL:Kohn CL is an author who researched nutritional support for patients with pancreatobiliary disease.BROZENEC S:Brozenec S is an author who contributed to the study of nutritional support for pancreatobiliary disease.FOSTER PF:Foster PF is an author involved in research on nutritional support for pancreatobiliary disease.MCCLAVE SA:McClave SA is a prominent author recognized for significant contributions to the field of nutrition support, particularly in the context of acute pancreatitis. They conducted a systematic review on nutrition support in this condition and have published various works addressing the topic. Additionally, McClave SA played a vital role in the consensus recommendations from the North American Surgical Nutrition Summit, further establishing their expertise in surgical nutrition. Furthermore, McClave SA is one of the authors involved in developing guidelines for nutrition support therapy in critically ill patients, highlighting their commitment to improving nutritional care in critical settings.CHANGE WK:Change WK is an author who contributed to the systematic review of nutrition support in acute pancreatitis.HEYLAND DK:Heyland DK is a prominent researcher recognized for significant contributions to clinical practice guidelines regarding nutrition support in critically ill patients. His work encompasses a broad understanding of nutritional management, particularly for vulnerable groups such as burn victims. Additionally, Heyland DK has played a key role as an author in the recommendations stemming from the North American Surgical Nutrition Summit. He has also contributed to the systematic review focused on nutrition support in cases of acute pancreatitis, further underscoring his expertise and influence in the field of nutritional care for critically ill individuals.MARIK PE:Marik PE is a researcher and author recognized for his significant contributions to the field of nutrition in acutely ill patients. He is particularly known for conducting systematic reviews on early enteral nutrition, which is crucial for the management of such patients. Additionally, Marik PE has authored a meta-analysis comparing parenteral and enteral nutrition specifically in cases of acute pancreatitis, further establishing his expertise in this area. His work includes multiple meta-analyses focused on nutrition strategies for patients suffering from acute pancreatitis, highlighting his commitment to improving clinical outcomes through evidence-based practices.ZALOGA GPL:Zaloga GPL is an author recognized for his contributions to the field of nutrition, particularly in the context of acute pancreatitis. He has collaborated on a meta-analysis that examines various nutrition methods applicable to this condition, as well as contributing to multiple studies focused on nutritional interventions in acute pancreatitis. His work highlights the importance of nutrition in managing this medical condition.WINDSOR AC:Windsor AC is an author who has conducted research on the effects of enteral feeding in acute pancreatitis. Through their studies, Windsor AC has contributed to the understanding of how enteral feeding impacts patients suffering from this condition.KANWAR S:Kanwar S is an author who contributed to the study comparing enteral and parenteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.LI AG:Li AG is an author involved in the research comparing enteral and parenteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.ABOU-ASSI S:Abou-Assi S is an author known for his research on hypocaloric jejunal feeding in the context of acute pancreatitis. His work includes a comparative study of hypocaloric jejunal feeding versus total parenteral nutrition, contributing valuable insights into nutritional strategies for managing this condition. Through his studies, Abou-Assi S has focused on the implications and effectiveness of hypocaloric jejunal feeding as a therapeutic approach in acute pancreatitis.CRAIG K:Craig K is an author who collaborated on the study of hypocaloric jejunal feeding in acute pancreatitis.O'KEEFE SJ:O'Keefe SJ is an author who has conducted significant research in the field of nutrition, particularly focusing on its role in the management of acute pancreatitis. He has specifically investigated hypocaloric jejunal feeding as a therapeutic approach in acute pancreatitis and has also explored nutritional strategies for managing necrotizing pancreatitis. Through his work, O'Keefe SJ contributes valuable insights into the nutritional management of these serious gastrointestinal conditions.POWELL JJ:Powell JJ is an author who conducted a trial on early enteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.MURCHISON JT:Murchison JT is an author who contributed to the trial of early enteral nutrition in acute pancreatitis.FEARON KC:Fearon KC is a researcher with a focus on optimizing nutritional support for cancer patients. In addition to this work, Fearon KC has contributed as an author to a consensus review concerning clinical care for patients undergoing colonic resection. Furthermore, Fearon KC has been involved in a trial investigating the effects of early enteral nutrition in cases of acute pancreatitis. Through these various roles, Fearon KC has made significant contributions to the field of nutritional support in clinical settings.LARVIN M:Larvin M is a researcher and author who has made significant contributions to the field of pancreatitis. He has conducted studies focused on treatments for chronic pancreatitis, exploring various therapeutic options to improve patient outcomes. Additionally, Larvin M has studied the APACHE-II score, a clinical tool used for assessing the severity of acute pancreatitis, further enhancing the understanding of this condition and its management. Through his research and publications, Larvin M has played a vital role in advancing knowledge in the area of pancreatitis.MCMAHON MJ:McMahon MJ is an author who contributed to the study of the APACHE-II score in acute pancreatitis.CORFIELD AP:Corfield AP is an author who researched the prediction of severity in acute pancreatitis.COOPER MJ:Cooper MJ is an author who collaborated on the prediction of severity in acute pancreatitis.WILLIAMSON RC:Williamson RC is an author involved in the prediction of severity in acute pancreatitis.KALFARENTZOS F:Kalfarentzos F is a researcher and author known for significant contributions to the field of enteral nutrition, particularly in the context of severe acute pancreatitis. He has conducted a randomized prospective trial focused on this area, furthering the understanding and application of enteral nutrition in patients suffering from this condition. His work encompasses various studies that explore the implications and effectiveness of enteral nutrition in managing severe acute pancreatitis, highlighting his expertise and commitment to advancing medical knowledge in this critical area.KEHAGIAS J:Kehagias J is an author who contributed to the research on enteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.MEAD N:Mead N is an author involved in the study of enteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.LOUIE B:Louie B is an author who assessed health technology regarding enteral or parenteral nutrition for severe pancreatitis.NOSEWORTHY T:Noseworthy T is an author who contributed to the health technology assessment of nutrition methods in severe pancreatitis.HAILEY D:Hailey D is an author involved in the health technology assessment of nutrition methods in severe pancreatitis.CASAS M:Casas M is an author who studied total enteral versus total parenteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.MORA J:Mora J is an author who collaborated on the study of nutrition methods in severe acute pancreatitis.FORT E:Fort E is an author involved in the research comparing total enteral and parenteral nutrition in severe acute pancreatitis.KAUSHIK N:Kaushik N is an author who studied enteral feeding without pancreatic stimulation.PIETRASZEWSKI M:Pietraszewski M is an author who contributed to the research on enteral feeding without pancreatic stimulation.HOLST JJ:Holst JJ is an author involved in the study of enteral feeding without pancreatic stimulation.ECKERWALL GE:Eckerwall GE is an author who researched immediate oral feeding in mild acute pancreatitis.TINGSTEDT BB:Tingstedt BB is an author who contributed to the study of immediate oral feeding in mild acute pancreatitis.BERGENZAUN PE:Bergenzaun PE is an author involved in the research on immediate oral feeding in mild acute pancreatitis.ANDER("ENTITY":EVENTHYPERCALORIC JEJUNAL FEEDING:Hypocaloric jejunal feeding is a nutritional strategy that provides fewer calories than the body needs, delivered through the jejunum, often used in patients with acute pancreatitis.RANDOMIZED CONTROLLED TRIAL:A randomized controlled trial is a study design that randomly assigns participants to different interventions to compare outcomes and assess the effects of those interventions. This methodology is often employed in clinical research to evaluate the effectiveness of various treatments. Specifically, randomized controlled trials are utilized to test the efficacy of nutritional interventions in patients, including those with gastric cancer. Additionally, this type of scientific experiment is used to examine the efficacy of treatments for conditions such as obesity, with examples including the testing of drugs like cetilistat and beloranib. Overall, randomized controlled trials are a crucial tool in determining the effectiveness of medical and nutritional interventions.SYSTEMATIC REVIEW:A systematic review is a methodical and comprehensive analysis of research studies focused on a specific topic. It serves as a crucial research method that summarizes the results of available studies, providing a thorough understanding of the evidence related to various subjects, particularly in health and nutritional research. Systematic reviews are essential for developing clinical practice guidelines and often address specific research questions. They can cover a wide range of topics, including but not limited to parenteral nutrition-associated cholestasis, dietary advice for women with gestational diabetes, nutrient deficiencies after bariatric surgery, the effects of diet on cognitive performance, and the impact of iron-fortified foods. By compiling and analyzing multiple studies, systematic reviews contribute significantly to the body of knowledge in their respective fields.META-ANALYSIS:META-ANALYSIS refers to a statistical technique that integrates the results of multiple studies to identify patterns, differences, or overall effects. This method is frequently utilized in dietary and health research to derive comprehensive conclusions from various research findings. By synthesizing data from different studies, meta-analysis helps to enhance the understanding of specific research questions and provides a more robust evidence base for decision-making in these fields.PROSPECTIVE STUDY:A prospective study is a research design that follows participants over time to observe outcomes, often used in clinical research to assess the effects of interventions.HEALTH TECHNOLOGY ASSESSMENT:Health technology assessment is a systematic evaluation of the properties and effects of health technology, including its direct and intended effects, as well as its indirect effects.CLINICAL TRIAL:A clinical trial is a research study conducted with human participants to evaluate the effects and efficacy of medical interventions. It tests how well new medical approaches, including new drugs, devices, or treatment strategies, work in people. Additionally, clinical trials can also focus on specific nutritional interventions to assess their impact on patient outcomes.INFLAMMATORY RESPONSE:The inflammatory response is a biological reaction that occurs in the body as a response to injury or infection. It can be particularly heightened in patients with esophageal cancer, where it contributes to conditions such as cachexia and weight loss. Additionally, the inflammatory response can be influenced by nutritional support, especially in cases of acute pancreatitis, highlighting the complex interplay between inflammation and nutritional status in various medical conditions.APACHE II SCORE:The APACHE II score is a severity-of-disease classification system that is used to assess the prognosis of patients in intensive care, particularly in cases like acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional support in acute pancreatitis refers to the strategies and methods used to provide adequate nutrition to patients suffering from this condition.SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Severe acute pancreatitis is a serious condition characterized by significant inflammation of the pancreas. This more severe form of acute pancreatitis can lead to various complications and necessitates intensive medical management. Patients with severe acute pancreatitis often require nutritional support to aid in their recovery.NUTRITIONAL INTERVENTIONS:Nutritional interventions are essential strategies designed to enhance dietary intake and improve nutritional status across various medical conditions. They are particularly recommended for patients with liver disease to help improve their nutritional status and prevent complications. These interventions are also crucial for individuals with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, where they aim to enhance overall health and dietary intake. Additionally, nutritional interventions play a significant role in the management of patients with acute pancreatitis, focusing on improving their nutritional status. Furthermore, these interventions often incorporate food education to promote better treatment adherence and healthier dietary patterns. Overall, nutritional interventions are vital in clinical settings to support patients' health and well-being.RANDOMIZED COMPARATIVE STUDY:A randomized comparative study is a type of research that compares two or more interventions in a randomized manner to determine their effectiveness.PROGNOSTIC INDICES:Prognostic indices are tools used to predict the outcome of a disease or condition, often based on clinical and laboratory data.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES:Nutritional outcomes refer to the results of nutritional interventions and assessments in patients with liver disease. These outcomes encompass improvements in health status, recovery times, and overall well-being, highlighting the significance of nutrition in the management and treatment of liver conditions.CLINICAL NUTRITION:Clinical Nutrition is a multifaceted field of study and practice that emphasizes the critical role of nutrition in clinical settings, particularly for patients with various health conditions, including cancer, diabetes, and acute pancreatitis. It encompasses the dietary management of patients to enhance health outcomes and improve disease management. 

In addition to being a specialized area of nutrition, Clinical Nutrition is also the title of a journal that publishes a wide range of research and reviews related to clinical nutrition practices. This journal covers topics such as home artificial nutrition, nutritional interventions in clinical settings, and the effects of nutrition on health outcomes. It includes studies on specific patient populations, such as those with stroke, pancreatitis, and burn care, as well as research on body mass index, malnutrition, and the prognostic significance of sarcopenia in hospitalized patients.

Overall, Clinical Nutrition serves as both a vital field of study and a key resource for practitioners, providing guidelines and evidence-based research to inform dietary management strategies in clinical practice.ENTERAL FEEDING:Enteral feeding is a method of delivering nutrition directly to the stomach or intestines, primarily utilized for patients who are unable to eat by mouth or cannot consume food normally. This approach ensures that individuals receive the necessary nutrients to maintain their health and support recovery when oral intake is not feasible.TOTAL PARENTERAL NUTRITION:Total parenteral nutrition (TPN) is a medical method of feeding that delivers nutrients directly into the bloodstream, bypassing the gastrointestinal tract. This approach is often utilized for patients who are unable to eat by mouth, including those with chylous conditions or severe cases of pancreatitis. TPN provides all nutritional requirements intravenously, making it a critical option for individuals who cannot obtain adequate nutrition through traditional means.DIETARY INTERVENTIONS:Dietary interventions refer to changes made to a patient's diet aimed at improving health outcomes. These interventions are particularly significant in the management of various diseases, including acute pancreatitis and chronic conditions such as Alzheimer's disease. By implementing strategies to modify dietary habits, dietary interventions seek to enhance overall health and well-being, addressing both acute and chronic health challenges.NUTRITIONAL THERAPY:Nutritional therapy is a comprehensive treatment approach that utilizes dietary interventions to enhance the quality of life and health outcomes for patients across various medical conditions. This therapy is particularly beneficial for individuals with cancer, liver disease, chronic kidney disease, and liver cirrhosis, as it involves tailored dietary practices aimed at managing these specific health issues. 

In addition to these conditions, nutritional therapy plays a crucial role in the recovery of patients with burns and is essential for managing diabetes by focusing on dietary adjustments to regulate blood sugar levels and minimize complications. It is also employed in the treatment of critically ill patients, addressing their unique nutritional needs to improve their clinical course.

Furthermore, nutritional therapy encompasses dietary management for patients with inflammatory bowel diseases, such as Crohn's disease, and is utilized in cases of acute pancreatitis. Overall, this therapy involves the provision of dietary prescriptions based on individual nutritional requirements, which may include various types of diets and nutritional support, making it a vital component of patient care in numerous health contexts.NUTRITIONAL EVALUATION:Nutritional evaluation is a critical process that involves assessing the dietary intake and nutritional status of patients across various demographics, including neonates, pediatric patients, and individuals with specific health conditions such as liver cirrhosis and diabetes. This evaluation is particularly significant in vulnerable populations, such as infants, where proper nutritional assessment can inform treatment plans and dietary interventions. The literature highlights the importance of nutritional evaluation in optimizing health outcomes, especially for those with liver disease and other chronic conditions. By understanding a patient's dietary needs and health status, healthcare providers can tailor interventions to improve overall nutritional health and support effective management of various medical conditions.NUTRITIONAL RESEARCH:Nutritional research is a scientific field dedicated to studying dietary practices and their effects on health. It particularly emphasizes the impact of nutrition on various health conditions, including liver disease and acute pancreatitis. This area of research aims to understand how diet and nutrition influence overall health and the progression of diseases, thereby contributing to improved dietary recommendations and interventions for patients suffering from these conditions.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional outcomes in acute pancreatitis refer to the results of nutritional interventions on patients suffering from this condition.NUTRITIONAL STRATEGIES IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional strategies in acute pancreatitis are specific approaches to providing nutrition to patients with this condition.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT GUIDELINES:Nutritional support guidelines are recommendations for providing nutrition to patients with specific medical conditions, including acute pancreatitis.NUTRITIONAL INTERVENTIONS IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional interventions in acute pancreatitis are specific strategies implemented to improve the nutritional status of affected patients.NUTRITIONAL THERAPY IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional therapy in acute pancreatitis involves dietary modifications and interventions aimed at managing the condition.NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT IN ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional assessment in acute pancreatitis is the evaluation of patients' dietary intake and nutritional needs to guide treatment.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES IN SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional outcomes in severe acute pancreatitis refer to the results of nutritional interventions on patients with this more serious form of the condition.NUTRITIONAL STRATEGIES IN SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional strategies in severe acute pancreatitis are specific approaches to providing nutrition to patients with this serious condition.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT IN SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional support in severe acute pancreatitis refers to the strategies and methods used to provide adequate nutrition to patients suffering from this serious condition.NUTRITIONAL GUIDELINES FOR ACUTE PANCREATITIS:Nutritional guidelines for acute pancreatitis are recommendations for dietary practices aimed at improving health outcomes in affected patients.NUTRITIONAL OUTCOMES IN PANCREATITIS:Nutritional outcomes in pancreatitis referDR. MAR√çA ARGENTE PLA:A medical doctor involved in a clinical case of a diabetic patient with cystic fibrosis.DR. ROSA M¬™ MIRETE L√ìPEZ:A medical doctor involved in a clinical case of a patient with diabetes mellitus and inflammatory bowel disease.GASTROSTOM√çA QUIR√öRGICA:GASTROSTOM√çA QUIR√öRGICA is a surgical procedure designed to create an opening into the stomach, primarily for the purpose of providing nutritional support to patients who experience difficulties with swallowing. This procedure involves the placement of a feeding tube directly into the stomach, which is particularly beneficial for individuals suffering from esophageal obstruction or other medical conditions that hinder the use of endoscopic techniques for feeding. Overall, GASTROSTOM√çA QUIR√öRGICA serves as a critical intervention for patients requiring alternative methods of nutrition delivery.PACIENTE CON DEMENCIA Y DIABETES MELLITUS TIPO 2:A clinical case involving a patient with dementia and type 2 diabetes mellitus, discussed during the round table event.PACIENTE DIAB√âTICO CON FIBROSIS QU√çSTICA:A clinical case involving a diabetic patient with cystic fibrosis, discussed during the round table event.PACIENTE CON DIABETES MELLITUS Y ENFERMEDAD INFLAMATORIA INTESTINAL:A clinical case involving a patient with diabetes mellitus and inflammatory bowel disease, discussed during the round table event.NUTRICI√ìN ENTERAL EN PACIENTE DIAB√âTICO CON CARCINOMA EPIDERMOIDE DE LARINGE:A clinical case involving enteral nutrition in a diabetic patient with laryngeal squamous cell carcinoma, discussed during the round table event.VAR√ìN DE 77 A√ëOS:A 77-year-old man with severe neurogenic dysphagia who was admitted to the neurology service due to a vertebrobasilar stroke.ENFERMEDAD ALZHEIMER:Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurological disorder that affects memory and cognitive function, diagnosed in the patient 14 months prior to admission.ICTUS VERTEBRO BASILAR:A vertebrobasilar stroke that the patient experienced, leading to his admission to the hospital.OLMESART√ÅN:Olmesartan is an oral medication prescribed to patients for managing hypertension, specifically used to treat high blood pressure.DIFICULTAD EN LA INGESTA ORAL:Oral intake difficulty is a condition affecting the patient's ability to consume food normally, leading to the need for nutritional support.TAC CEREBRAL:TAC cerebral is a medical imaging procedure used to diagnose conditions such as ischemic stroke, specifically in this case, the right PICA ischemic stroke.ICTUS ISQU√âMICO:Ictus isqu√©mico refers to a type of stroke that occurs due to a blockage in blood flow to the brain. This interruption in circulation can result in potential brain damage, highlighting the critical nature of timely medical intervention in such cases.UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA (UNCYD):UNCyD is a clinical nutrition and dietetics unit that provides nutritional assessment and support for patients.FG CKD-EPI:FG CKD-EPI is a formula used to estimate the glomerular filtration rate, indicating kidney function.PRO-BNP:Pro-BNP is a biomarker used to diagnose heart failure and assess cardiac function.HEMOGRAMA:Hemograma is a complete blood count test that evaluates overall health and detects a variety of disorders.GASOMETR√çA VENOSA:Gasometr√≠a venosa is a medical test that measures the levels of gases in the blood, specifically assessing respiratory and metabolic function. This venous blood gas analysis is particularly useful for evaluating the acid-base balance and oxygenation in patients, including those with eating disorders.COAGULACI√ìN:Coagulaci√≥n tests evaluate the blood's ability to clot, important for assessing bleeding disorders.RADIOGRAF√çA DE T√ìRAX:RADIOGRAF√çA DE T√ìRAX refers to a chest X-ray that is utilized to visualize the lungs and heart, allowing for the evaluation of lung health and the detection of any abnormalities or complications. This imaging technique is particularly important in assessing conditions related to eating disorders, as it helps identify potential health issues that may arise in the lungs.FLUIDOTERAPIA INTRAVENOSA:Fluidoterapia intravenosa is a medical treatment involving the administration of fluids directly into the bloodstream to maintain hydration and electrolyte balance.CIRCUNFERENCIA DE PANTORRILLA:Circunferencia de pantorrilla is a measurement of the calf used to assess nutritional status and muscle mass.HIPOINGESTA:Hipoingesta refers to a state of reduced food intake, which can lead to weight loss and nutritional deficiencies.RESULTADOS ANAL√çTICOS:Resultados anal√≠ticos refer to the outcomes of various medical tests conducted to evaluate a patient's health.DESCOMPENSACI√ìN HIPERGLUC√âMICA:Descompensaci√≥n hipergluc√©mica refers to a state of elevated blood sugar levels, often requiring medical intervention.UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN Y DIET√âTICA:Unidad especializada en la valoraci√≥n y soporte nutricional de pacientes con desnutrici√≥n y otras condiciones relacionadas con la alimentaci√≥nDIFICULTAD PARA INGESTA:Problema que presenta el paciente, que requiere incentivaci√≥n continua para terminar los alimentosCCEE UNCYD:CCEE UNCyD is a clinical center involved in nutritional support and clinical evolution monitoring for patients with specific dietary needs.NUTRITION ENTERAL:Nutrition enteral (NE) is a method of providing nutrition directly to the gastrointestinal tract, which is particularly beneficial for patients who have undergone surgery or are suffering from burns. This approach involves delivering nutrition directly to the stomach or intestine through a tube, ensuring that the body receives essential nutrients efficiently. The physiological benefits of enteral nutrition make it a preferred choice in clinical settings, especially for patients recovering from surgical procedures or those with specific dietary needs.ICTUS VERTEBRO-BASILAR:A vertebrobasilar stroke is a type of stroke that affects the brainstem and can lead to various complications, including dysphagia.DIETA HIPERCAL√ìRICA:The "DIETA HIPERCAL√ìRICA" refers to a hypercaloric diet, which is a high-calorie dietary regimen specifically designed to provide an increased energy intake. This type of diet is often preferred for patients who require volume restriction, making it suitable for individuals with specific health needs. Additionally, it is commonly utilized for patients experiencing malnutrition, as it aims to help them gain weight and improve their overall nutritional status.TRATAMIENTO ANTIDEPRESIVO:Antidepressant treatment is a medical intervention aimed at managing symptoms of depression, which can affect a patient's appetite and nutritional intake.VAR√ìN DE 76 A√ëOS:NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL TOTAL CENTRAL:Nutritional therapy administered through a central venous access to provide essential nutrients to the patient during hospitalizationNUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL PERIF√âRICA:NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL PERIF√âRICA (NPP) es un m√©todo de administraci√≥n de nutrientes que se realiza a trav√©s de una v√≠a perif√©rica. Este enfoque es utilizado en pacientes que no pueden recibir alimentaci√≥n por v√≠a enteral, asegurando as√≠ que se mantenga su estado nutricional. La NPP es especialmente relevante en situaciones donde se anticipa la necesidad de una alimentaci√≥n m√°s prolongada, como antes de la colocaci√≥n de una gastrostom√≠a.SERVICIO DE CIRUG√çA:The "Servicio de Cirug√≠a" is a specialized unit within a hospital dedicated to conducting surgical procedures and providing postoperative care. This department is responsible for evaluating patients and performing various surgical interventions, including the gastrostomy procedure.UNIDAD DE ENDOSCOP√çAS:Department where endoscopic procedures are performed, including attempts to place a gastrostomyCORTICOIDES IV:Intravenous corticosteroids administered to the patient as part of the treatment regimenANTIBIOTICOTERAPIA IV:Intravenous antibiotic therapy provided to the patient to prevent or treat infectionsRADIOLOG√çA INTERVENCIONISTA:Radiolog√≠a Intervencionista is a medical specialty that focuses on utilizing imaging techniques to guide minimally invasive surgical procedures. This specialty is often essential for various techniques, including gastrostomy and yeyunostomy. Within this context, the interventional radiology department has actively engaged in procedures such as the placement of a gastrostomy tube for patients, demonstrating its critical role in providing advanced medical care through minimally invasive methods.FLUIDOTERAPIA IV:CEREBRAL HOSPITAL:El hospital de da√±o cerebral (HDC) es un centro especializado donde se contin√∫a el tratamiento y rehabilitaci√≥n de pacientes con da√±o neurol√≥gico.MEC:MEC is a scale used to assess the level of dementia in patients.GDS:GDS is a scale used to measure the severity of dementia.BARTHEL:Barthel is a scale used to measure the degree of dependence in patients.FAMILY:Family refers to the relatives of the patient who are involved in decision-making regarding the patient's care and treatment.ENDOCINOLOG√çA Y NUTRICI√ìN:Endocrinolog√≠a y nutrici√≥n son servicios m√©dicos que colaboran en el tratamiento de pacientes con problemas nutricionales.DIFICULTAD EN EL CONTROL GLUC√âMICO:La dificultad en el control gluc√©mico se refiere a los problemas que enfrentan los pacientes diab√©ticos para mantener niveles adecuados de glucosa en sangre.ACCIDENTE CEREBRO VASCULAR:HOSPITAL UNIVERSITARIO Y POLIT√âCNICO DE LA FE:Hospital Universitario y Polit√©cnico de La Fe is a reference hospital in Valencia for nutritional assessment and treatment.F508DEL:F508del is a specific mutation associated with cystic fibrosis that the patient carries.DIABETES RELACIONADA CON LA FIBROSIS QU√çSTICA:Diabetes related to cystic fibrosis (DRFQ) is a significant health condition that affects individuals with cystic fibrosis. It is recognized as the most common non-respiratory complication in cystic fibrosis patients, with a prevalence of approximately 20%. One patient has been managing this diabetes condition since the age of 14, necessitating insulin treatment to help control their blood sugar levels. However, this patient has experienced poor glycemic control, highlighting the challenges associated with managing diabetes in the context of cystic fibrosis.DESNUTRICI√ìN CAL√ìRICA CR√ìNICA GRAVE:A severe chronic caloric malnutrition condition diagnosed in the patient, requiring specialized dietary management.RETRASO DEL CRECIMIENTO:"RETRASO DEL CRECIMIENTO" refers to a condition indicating that the patient is not growing at the expected rate, which is often associated with underlying health issues. This growth delay is characterized by an individual not reaching expected growth milestones, and it is frequently observed in cases of metabolic disorders.(N␀␀␀␀␀␀ñN␀␀␀␀␀␀O␀␀␀␀␀␀lO␀␀␀␀␀␀‘O␀␀␀␀␀␀LP␀␀␀␀␀␀¿P␀␀␀␀␀␀2Q␀␀␀␀␀␀¢Q␀␀␀␀␀␀R␀␀␀␀␀␀~R␀␀␀␀␀␀pS␀␀␀␀␀␀ºT␀␀␀␀␀␀W␀␀␀␀␀␀ßW␀␀␀␀␀␀FX␀␀␀␀␀␀Y␀␀␀␀␀␀lY␀␀␀␀␀␀Û[␀␀␀␀␀␀x\␀␀␀␀␀␀Û\␀␀␀␀␀␀ß^␀␀␀␀␀␀%_␀␀␀␀␀␀Û`␀␀␀␀␀␀Üa␀␀␀␀␀␀2b␀␀␀␀␀␀€b␀␀␀␀␀␀\c␀␀␀␀␀␀∆c␀␀␀␀␀␀e␀␀␀␀␀␀f␀␀␀␀␀␀µf␀␀␀␀␀␀Øh␀␀␀␀␀␀3i␀␀␀␀␀␀ªi␀␀␀␀␀␀Bj␀␀␀␀␀␀Ym␀␀␀␀␀␀Ûm␀␀␀␀␀␀[n␀␀␀␀␀␀‹n␀␀␀␀␀␀Oo␀␀␀␀␀␀⁄o␀␀␀␀␀␀Yp␀␀␀␀␀␀√p␀␀␀␀␀␀€p␀␀␀␀␀␀vq␀␀␀␀␀␀›s␀␀␀␀␀␀Üt␀␀␀␀␀␀#u␀␀␀␀␀␀w␀␀␀␀␀␀≤w␀␀␀␀␀␀Ñz␀␀␀␀␀␀{␀␀␀␀␀␀—|␀␀␀␀␀␀∫}␀␀␀␀␀␀≤~␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä␀␀␀␀␀␀bÄ␀␀␀␀␀␀ëÇ␀␀␀␀␀␀iÉ␀␀␀␀␀␀¬Ü␀␀␀␀␀␀*ä␀␀␀␀␀␀›ä␀␀␀␀␀␀Ñã␀␀␀␀␀␀)å␀␀␀␀␀␀Ïç␀␀␀␀␀␀tè␀␀␀␀␀␀ºë␀␀␀␀␀␀Zí␀␀␀␀␀␀ıí␀␀␀␀␀␀¡î␀␀␀␀␀␀¬ò␀␀␀␀␀␀\ô␀␀␀␀␀␀”ô␀␀␀␀␀␀eö␀␀␀␀␀␀Êö␀␀␀␀␀␀Pú␀␀␀␀␀␀Íú␀␀␀␀␀␀Wù␀␀␀␀␀␀ƒù␀␀␀␀␀␀?û␀␀␀␀␀␀Øû␀␀␀␀␀␀'ü␀␀␀␀␀␀≤ü␀␀␀␀␀␀	†␀␀␀␀␀␀v†␀␀␀␀␀␀à¢␀␀␀␀␀␀£␀␀␀␀␀␀Ø£␀␀␀␀␀␀a•␀␀␀␀␀␀Á•␀␀␀␀␀␀a¶␀␀␀␀␀␀˚¶␀␀␀␀␀␀\©␀␀␀␀␀␀∫©␀␀␀␀␀␀ ≠␀␀␀␀␀␀XÆ␀␀␀␀␀␀Ø␀␀␀␀␀␀–Ø␀␀␀␀␀␀¨∞␀␀␀␀␀␀/±␀␀␀␀␀␀≠±␀␀␀␀␀␀	≤␀␀␀␀␀␀Å≤␀␀␀␀␀␀Ì≤␀␀␀␀␀␀m≥␀␀␀␀␀␀“≥␀␀␀␀␀␀E¥␀␀␀␀␀␀ß¥␀␀␀␀␀␀#µ␀␀␀␀␀␀éµ␀␀␀␀␀␀∂␀␀␀␀␀␀v∂␀␀␀␀␀␀Í∂␀␀␀␀␀␀P∑␀␀␀␀␀␀”∑␀␀␀␀␀␀á∏␀␀␀␀␀␀(π␀␀␀␀␀␀ π␀␀␀␀␀␀j∫␀␀␀␀␀␀	ª␀␀␀␀␀␀∂ª␀␀␀␀␀␀[º␀␀␀␀␀␀
